/* GENERATED by tools/build-profiles.js from data/profiles/*.json. Do not edit by hand. */
(window.MP = window.MP || {}).Profiles = {"wonder":{"id":"wonder","intro":"You are Stevland Morris from Detroit, blind since birth, with a head full of melodies. School, travel and contracts all assume someone else knows best, and you spend years winning the right to decide for yourself.","zones":{"ladder":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e1","why":"School: blind children of his time were taught in separate schools for the blind."},"office":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e2","why":"Contracts: until 21 others controlled his contract and money."},"market":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e2","why":"Business: his earnings and his songs were not his to control."},"bridge":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e3","why":"Support: his family, Clarence Paul and Motown backed him."},"quiet":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e4","why":"Studio: he played many instruments himself on his classic albums."}},"advantage":{"type":"skip","uses":3,"title":"Learning by ear","text":"Stevie learned songs and arrangements by ear and played many instruments himself. On a question you know by heart, count it as answered right.","ev":"e4"},"life":[{"k":"info","title":"Saginaw, 1950","text":"Stevie is born six weeks early. In the incubator, too much oxygen harms his eyes (retinopathy of prematurity) and he is blind. His mother Lula Mae soon moves the family to Detroit."},{"k":"barrier","title":"Decided for him","text":"Many adults expect a blind child to have a small life. School, travel and play need extra planning, and people often decide for him what he is able to do.","move":-1},{"k":"boost","title":"Music everywhere","text":"He teaches himself harmonica, piano and drums as a small child and sings in the church choir. Later he attends the Michigan School for the Blind.","move":1},{"k":"boost","title":"Discovered at eleven","text":"Ronnie White of The Miracles brings him to Motown. Founder Berry Gordy signs him as \"Little Stevie Wonder\". A tutor goes along, and his mother reads his contracts.","move":2},{"k":"info","title":"Number one at 13","text":"\"Fingertips\" (1963), recorded live, reaches number one. It is the first live album to top the US chart. Much of his money is held in trust until he turns 21."},{"k":"barrier","title":"Whose songs?","text":"As a teenager, Stevie's songs are chosen for him by the label. What he wants to say about racism, poverty and love is not what the company expects from a pop star.","move":-1},{"k":"quiz","title":"Turning 21","text":"In 1971, at 21, Stevie refuses to sign the same kind of contract again. He negotiates a new deal, against the company's expectations.","move":1,"q":{"q":"What did Stevie win in his new contract at 21?","a":["Control over his own music","A permanent seat on the board","A ban on touring"],"x":"He gained artistic control: he chose the songs, played most instruments himself and explored synthesizers."}},{"k":"boost","title":"The classic albums","text":"With control he makes Music of My Mind, Talking Book and Innervisions, playing most instruments himself and exploring synthesizers. His songs speak about racism and poverty.","move":1},{"k":"barrier","title":"August 1973: the crash","text":"A car crash in North Carolina leaves him in a coma for days. He survives, but loses most of his sense of smell. Doctors fear he may not make music again.","move":-2},{"k":"boost","title":"Songs in the Key of Life","text":"His 1976 double album is a worldwide success. Over his career he wins 25 Grammy Awards, including three for Album of the Year.","move":1},{"k":"quiz","title":"A song for a holiday","text":"He writes \"Happy Birthday\" (1980) for Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. and campaigns for a national holiday. In 1981 he leads a rally in Washington, and in 1983 the law is signed.","move":1,"q":{"q":"Which holiday did Stevie's campaign help create in the USA?","a":["Martin Luther King Jr. Day","Thanksgiving","Independence Day"],"x":"The holiday was signed into law in 1983 and first observed in 1986."}},{"k":"info","title":"Speaking up worldwide","text":"He speaks out against apartheid, and his music is banned in South Africa. He becomes a UN Messenger of Peace in 2009 and supports the Marrakesh Treaty, which gives blind readers more access to books."}],"sources":["js/characters/group1.js tiles for wonder","docs/characters.md row for Stevie Wonder","Rock & Roll Hall of Fame: Stevie Wonder","Martin Luther King Jr. Research & Education Institute","UN Messenger of Peace profile"],"flags":["Check that he campaigned for the Marrakesh Treaty (adopted 2013); the single-player data says he later supported it.","The market zone is an access argument (noise and no guide); a human may want to confirm that framing."],"evidence":[{"id":"e1","y":"1950s","ev":"Born premature and blind, he went to the Michigan School for the Blind, one of the separate schools that blind children were sent to at the time.","src":2},{"id":"e2","y":"1961 to 1971","ev":"Signed to Motown at 11; his contract held his earnings in trust until 21, and at 21 he renegotiated and gained creative control.","src":2},{"id":"e3","y":"1960s","ev":"Motown's Berry Gordy, mentor Clarence Paul and his family supported his career.","src":2},{"id":"e4","y":"1972 to 1976","ev":"He recorded a run of classic albums, playing many instruments himself.","src":2}],"balance":{"comp":11},"look":{"skin":"#7a5238","hair":"#1a1410","hairStyle":"braids","top":"#26262e","bottom":"#26262e","outfit":"suit","prop":"sunglasses"}},"king":{"id":"king","intro":"You grow up in a close, church-centred family in segregated Atlanta, and you learn to speak to a crowd. Your tools are words, organising and a movement of thousands; your opponents are laws, threats and an FBI that watches you.","zones":{"office":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e1","why":"Law: segregation laws decided where he could sit, eat and learn."},"rope":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e2","why":"Safety: a bomb at home, a stabbing, jail and FBI surveillance."},"bridge":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e3","why":"Church and family: a community that carried the movement."},"ladder":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e4","why":"Education: Morehouse at 15 and a doctorate opened a path for his voice."}},"advantage":{"type":"shield","uses":3,"title":"Staying power","text":"The movement held after bombings, arrests and setbacks. A wrong step does not send you back: others pick up the work and you stay in place.","ev":"e3"},"life":[{"k":"info","title":"Atlanta, 1929","text":"Born on 15 January 1929 as Michael King Jr. into a family of pastors; his father later changes both their names to Martin. The home is comfortable, but outside it segregation laws shape every part of life."},{"k":"barrier","title":"“You can’t play with us”","text":"As a small child, Martin is told his white friend can no longer play with him. His parents explain that racism exists, and that he must never think he is “less”.","move":-1},{"k":"barrier","title":"The bus ride home","text":"At 14, after winning a speech contest, Martin rides home by bus. The driver orders him and his teacher to give up their seats to white passengers, and they stand for about 90 miles. He later calls it the angriest night of his life.","move":-1},{"k":"boost","title":"College at 15","text":"Martin enters Morehouse College at 15. His teachers show him that faith and the fight for justice belong together.","move":2},{"k":"quiz","title":"Learning from Gandhi","text":"In seminary and graduate school, Martin studies how others have resisted injustice. One leader from India shapes his ideas of peaceful protest.","q":{"q":"Whose nonviolent resistance inspired King’s approach?","a":["Mahatma Gandhi","Napoleon","Winston Churchill"],"x":"Gandhi led nonviolent campaigns against British rule in India. King adapted these ideas for the American civil rights movement."},"move":1},{"k":"boost","title":"Montgomery Bus Boycott","text":"In December 1955 the 26-year-old pastor is chosen to lead the Montgomery Improvement Association. For 381 days thousands of people refuse the buses, and the segregation law falls.","move":2},{"k":"barrier","title":"Bomb at the front door","text":"On 30 January 1956 a bomb explodes at the King home while Coretta and their baby are inside. No one is hurt. Martin asks the angry crowd outside not to answer violence with violence.","move":-1},{"k":"barrier","title":"Jail in Birmingham","text":"In April 1963 Martin is arrested during protests against segregation in Birmingham, Alabama. From his cell he writes a letter explaining why waiting for justice is not an option.","move":-1},{"k":"boost","title":"“I Have a Dream”","text":"On 28 August 1963 about 250,000 people gather in Washington for jobs and freedom. Martin gives his famous speech at the Lincoln Memorial, one voice among many organisers and speakers.","move":2},{"k":"quiz","title":"Laws change","text":"The Civil Rights Act of 1964 bans segregation in public places and discrimination at work. After the marches in Selma, another law follows. At 35 Martin receives the Nobel Peace Prize.","q":{"q":"Which 1965 law protected the right to vote?","a":["The Voting Rights Act","The Emancipation Proclamation","The Civil Rights Act of 1964"],"x":"The Voting Rights Act of 1965 followed the Selma marches and targeted barriers like literacy tests that kept Black citizens from voting."},"move":1},{"k":"barrier","title":"A harder fight","text":"Martin speaks out against the Vietnam War and for poor people of every race. Many allies and politicians turn away, and the FBI watches him and tries to damage his name.","move":-1},{"k":"info","title":"Memphis, 1968 and after","text":"Martin goes to Memphis to support striking sanitation workers. On 4 April 1968 he is assassinated, aged 39. Since 1986 the US marks Martin Luther King Jr. Day, and the movement’s laws still protect millions."}],"sources":["js/characters/group1.js (king tiles)","King Institute, Stanford (kinginstitute.stanford.edu)","Nobel Prize: Martin Luther King Jr. biography"],"flags":["Ages and dates follow the single-player tiles; 90-mile bus ride and 'angriest night' wording from King's own recollection as cited there."],"evidence":[{"id":"e1","y":"1929 to 1944","ev":"Segregation laws shaped his childhood in Atlanta; on a bus ride at 15 he was made to stand for about 90 miles.","src":1},{"id":"e2","y":"1956 to 1968","ev":"His house was bombed in 1956, he was stabbed in 1958, jailed in Birmingham in 1963 and watched by the FBI.","src":1},{"id":"e3","y":"1940s to 1950s","ev":"His family and the Ebenezer Baptist Church were a strong base for him.","src":1},{"id":"e4","y":"1944 to 1955","ev":"He entered Morehouse College at 15 and earned a doctorate at Boston University in 1955.","src":2}],"balance":{"comp":21},"look":{"skin":"#6b4a33","hair":"#1a1410","hairStyle":"short","top":"#26262e","bottom":"#26262e","outfit":"suit","prop":"none"}},"kaepernick":{"id":"kaepernick","intro":"You are the quarterback everyone watches, and often the only Black kid in the room: adopted into a white family, drilled in training, valued for your arm while people assume things about you. When you decide to speak, the whole league can hear it.","zones":{"rope":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e2","why":"Backlash: boos, burned jerseys and threats after the kneeling protest."},"market":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e3","why":"Work: after 2016 no team signed him, though he was still good enough to play."},"sprint":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e1","why":"Sport: at the top of his game he led a team to the Super Bowl."},"bridge":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e4","why":"Community: the Know Your Rights Camp and fellow players stood with him."}},"advantage":{"type":"bestof2","uses":3,"title":"Training camp","text":"Elite training: he reached the Super Bowl in 2013 through years of drilled preparation. Roll two dice and keep the higher one.","ev":"e1"},"life":[{"k":"info","title":"Milwaukee, 1987","text":"Colin is born on 3 November 1987 to a white mother and a Black father who is not part of his life. At five weeks he is adopted by Rick and Teresa Kaepernick and grows up in California. He speaks warmly of his adoptive family."},{"k":"barrier","title":"Hair and being judged","text":"As a teen with braids, Colin is pushed by adults to change his hair so he looks “respectable”. He is often the only Black player on his teams, and stereotypes follow him. The series Colin in Black & White dramatises this.","move":-1},{"k":"barrier","title":"Few offers, one yes","text":"Colin shines at baseball and as a quarterback, but gets few scholarship offers to play quarterback. Only the University of Nevada offers him that place.","move":-1},{"k":"boost","title":"To the NFL","text":"At Nevada he breaks records, and in 2011 the San Francisco 49ers draft him in the second round. In February 2013 he leads the team to Super Bowl XLVII.","move":2},{"k":"info","title":"Watching the news","text":"In 2016 killings of Black men by police dominate the news. Colin reads about them and about American history, and decides he cannot show pride in a flag for a country that, in his view, oppresses Black people."},{"k":"quiz","title":"Sit, then kneel","text":"Colin first sits during the anthem in an August 2016 preseason game. After talking with a former soldier, he changes his way of protesting to something he sees as more respectful.","q":{"q":"Which gesture did Kaepernick settle on after talking with Nate Boyer?","a":["Kneeling during the anthem","Leaving the stadium","Raising a banner"],"x":"Boyer, a former Green Beret, suggested kneeling as a respectful gesture. The protest spread to other players and divided the country."},"move":1},{"k":"barrier","title":"Backlash","text":"Colin is booed, receives death threats and is called unpatriotic by many. Others, including many players and veterans, support him. He leaves the 49ers after 2016.","move":-1},{"k":"barrier","title":"No team calls","text":"In 2017 Colin is a free agent, but no NFL team signs him, and he has not played in the league since. He files a grievance accusing the league of collusion, which is settled in 2019.","move":-1},{"k":"boost","title":"Know Your Rights Camp","text":"Colin donates large sums and founds a camp for young people that teaches their legal rights, financial literacy and how to handle police encounters.","move":3},{"k":"quiz","title":"What does the camp teach?","text":"The camp is built around practical knowledge, not slogans. Young people leave knowing what to do and who to call.","q":{"q":"What is a main aim of the Know Your Rights Camp?","a":["Teaching young people about their legal rights","Training new quarterbacks","Selling sports gear"],"x":"The camp teaches legal rights, financial literacy and how to act in encounters with police."},"move":1},{"k":"boost","title":"“Believe in something”","text":"In 2018 Nike makes Colin the face of a campaign: “Believe in something. Even if it means sacrificing everything.” In 2021 the series Colin in Black & White, co-created with Ava DuVernay, tells his teenage years.","move":3},{"k":"info","title":"A conversation that continues","text":"Colin remains a prominent and polarising figure. Some see someone who gave up football for justice; others disagree with the protest. His story asks how much speaking up costs, and who pays."}],"sources":["js/characters/group1.js (kaepernick tiles)","Netflix: Colin in Black & White (2021), dramatised","Know Your Rights Camp (knowyourrightscamp.com)","Press coverage of the 2016 protest and 2019 settlement"],"flags":["Contemporary and polarising figure: wording kept factual and balanced; please have someone review the tone.","Series scenes (hair, recruiting) are dramatised; recruiting detail follows the single-player data."],"evidence":[{"id":"e1","y":"2013","ev":"He was quarterback of the 49ers in the Super Bowl XLVII, a game he led to the final minutes.","src":3},{"id":"e2","y":"2016","ev":"After he sat and then knelt during the anthem to protest police violence, he faced boos, burned jerseys and threats.","src":3},{"id":"e3","y":"2017 to 2019","ev":"No NFL team signed him after the 2016 season; his collusion grievance was settled in 2019.","src":3},{"id":"e4","y":"2016","ev":"He founded the Know Your Rights Camp for young people.","src":2}],"balance":{"comp":15},"look":{"skin":"#9a6a4a","hair":"#1a1410","hairStyle":"afro","top":"#c8102e","bottom":"#e5d9a8","outfit":"football","prop":"helmet"}},"curie":{"id":"curie","intro":"You are Maria Skłodowska, a top student in a Warsaw where Polish is suppressed and women may not enter university. Doors are locked by law, so you study in secret, then in Paris, and then in a leaky shed full of ore.","zones":{"ladder":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e1","why":"Education: in Russian-ruled Warsaw women were barred from university and Polish was banned."},"office":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e5","why":"Institutions: the Academy of Sciences refused to admit her by a narrow vote."},"market":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e6","why":"Press and public: in 1911 newspapers attacked her with sexist and xenophobic language."},"bridge":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e7","why":"Partnership: Pierre insisted that she be named with him for the Nobel Prize."}},"advantage":{"type":"hint","uses":2,"title":"Method and notebooks","text":"Curie solved problems by careful measurement and by ruling out what could not be true. Use it to remove one wrong answer before you decide.","ev":"e4"},"life":[{"k":"info","title":"Warsaw, 1867","text":"Maria is born on 7 November 1867, the youngest of five children of two teachers. Poland is ruled by Russia, and Polish language and history are banned from the classroom."},{"k":"barrier","title":"Loss at ten","text":"Her sister Zofia dies of typhus in 1876 and her mother of tuberculosis in 1878. Her father loses his post for his Polish views, so money is short for the whole family.","move":-1},{"k":"boost","title":"Gold medal at fifteen","text":"Maria finishes school as the best in her class and receives a gold medal. Her parents, both teachers, had made learning the centre of the home.","move":1},{"k":"barrier","title":"A university that says no","text":"The University of Warsaw does not accept women. Maria and her sister Bronisława study at the secret \"Flying University\" and agree to take turns: Maria works first so Bronisława can study medicine in Paris.","move":-1},{"k":"quiz","title":"The Flying University","text":"Teachers moved from flat to flat so that women could be taught without police noticing. Maria learned science and also taught others.","move":1,"q":{"q":"What was the \"Flying University\" in Warsaw?","a":["A secret school where women could study","A university that taught on trains","A school for airplane engineers"],"x":"It was an underground school for Polish women, held in private flats and moved often because Russian authorities did not allow it."}},{"k":"boost","title":"Paris, 1891","text":"At 24 Maria enrols at the Sorbonne as \"Marie\". She lives in a cold attic and sometimes forgets to eat, but passes physics in 1893 and mathematics in 1894.","move":1},{"k":"info","title":"A partner in the lab","text":"In 1895 Marie marries the physicist Pierre Curie. They share ideas, notes and work as equals, and later write their results under both names."},{"k":"quiz","title":"Polonium and radium","text":"In 1898 Marie and Pierre announce two new elements. She coins the word \"radioactivity\". They work in a leaky shed and process tons of ore by hand.","move":1,"q":{"q":"Marie named one new element after her homeland. Which?","a":["Polonium","Radium","Becquerelium"],"x":"Polonium is named for Poland, which did not exist as an independent state when she named it in 1898."}},{"k":"boost","title":"Nobel Prize, 1903","text":"Marie and Pierre share the Nobel Prize in Physics with Henri Becquerel. She is the first woman to win a Nobel Prize. They choose not to patent their method so that other scientists can use it.","move":1},{"k":"barrier","title":"Alone, then in the headlines","text":"Pierre is killed in a street accident in 1906, and Marie takes over his chair as the first woman professor at the Sorbonne. In 1911 the French Academy rejects her narrowly and the press attacks her as a foreigner.","move":-1},{"k":"boost","title":"The Little Curies","text":"In 1911 she also wins a second Nobel Prize, in Chemistry. In the First World War she equips vehicles with X-ray machines and trains women to run them, so surgeons can find bullets and shrapnel.","move":2},{"k":"info","title":"A lab for the world","text":"Marie builds the Radium Institute in Paris and trains many researchers, many of them women. Her daughter Irène wins the Chemistry Nobel in 1935. Marie dies in 1934, probably of radiation exposure."}],"sources":["js/characters/group1.js tiles for curie","docs/characters.md row for Marie Curie","Nobel Prize site: Marie Curie biographical","Musée Curie"],"flags":["Check wording that the 1911 Academy vote was narrowly lost, and that the Flying University used private flats and rotating teachers.","Dates for Pierre's 1906 chair and the first woman professor at the Sorbonne were taken from the single-player data."],"evidence":[{"id":"e1","y":"1880s","ev":"Warsaw was under Russian rule: Polish was suppressed in schools, and women could not attend university.","src":3},{"id":"e2","y":"1880s","ev":"She joined the secret Flying University in Warsaw, where Polish women studied in private flats.","src":3},{"id":"e3","y":"1891 to 1894","ev":"She enrolled at the Sorbonne in Paris in 1891 and earned degrees in physics and mathematics.","src":2},{"id":"e4","y":"1898 to 1902","ev":"With Pierre she processed tonnes of pitchblende in a leaky shed to isolate radium.","src":3},{"id":"e5","y":"1911","ev":"The French Academy of Sciences narrowly voted against her membership in 1911; it elected its first woman full member only in 1979.","src":3},{"id":"e6","y":"1911","ev":"A press campaign around the Langevin affair attacked her, often with xenophobic and sexist language.","src":2},{"id":"e7","y":"1903","ev":"Pierre made sure she was named with him for the Nobel Prize in Physics after the first nomination left her out.","src":2}],"balance":{"comp":20},"look":{"skin":"#f0d2b0","hair":"#7a5b3a","hairStyle":"bun","top":"#2c2c34","bottom":"#2c2c34","outfit":"labcoat","prop":"flask"}},"heumann":{"id":"heumann","intro":"You are Judy, a wheelchair user from Brooklyn who grew up in the 1950s, when schools could simply say no. Steps and locked doors are everywhere, but so are the rules, forms and crowds of people that you will learn to use better than most.","zones":{"stairs":{"fx":"block","ev":"e2","why":"Steps and transport: buses, buildings and planes were built without wheelchair access."},"ladder":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e1","why":"School: she was refused entry as 'a fire hazard' and taught at home for years."},"office":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e3","why":"Licences and rules: New York refused her teaching licence until she sued."},"bridge":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e5","why":"Allies: other disabled activists, even the Black Panthers, stood with her in 1977."}},"advantage":{"type":"bestof2","uses":2,"title":"Second push","text":"Judy kept going: she sued over her teaching licence in 1970 and led the 1977 sit-in. Roll two dice and take the higher one.","ev":"e5"},"life":[{"k":"info","title":"Brooklyn, 1947","text":"Judy is born on 18 December 1947 to German-Jewish parents who had escaped the Nazis. At 18 months she gets polio and begins to use a wheelchair. She grows up in Brooklyn, New York."},{"k":"barrier","title":"\"A fire hazard\"","text":"In 1953 her local school refuses to let her attend kindergarten: a wheelchair, it says, is a fire hazard. Her mother Ilse fights the decision, but for years Judy only gets a few hours of home teaching.","move":-3},{"k":"boost","title":"Camp Jened","text":"At a summer camp for disabled teenagers in the Catskills, Judy sees a place where disability is normal and decisions are shared. Friendships from camp later grow into activism, and the film Crip Camp tells that story.","move":1},{"k":"quiz","title":"The teaching licence","text":"In 1970 New York City refuses Judy a teaching licence because she cannot walk. She takes the city to court.","move":1,"q":{"q":"What happened when Judy challenged the city's refusal?","a":["She won and became the first wheelchair user to teach in New York City schools","The court agreed with the city and she gave up teaching","The city offered her only a desk job without students"],"x":"Judy won her case in 1970. The news made her widely known, and she taught in New York City public schools."}},{"k":"info","title":"Disabled in Action","text":"In 1970 Judy helps found Disabled in Action, a group led by disabled people. Later she moves to Berkeley, California, a centre of the independent living movement, where disabled people run their own services."},{"k":"barrier","title":"A law on paper only","text":"Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act (1973) bans discrimination by programmes that get government money. But the government keeps delaying the rules that would make it real, and nothing changes in schools or buildings.","move":-3},{"k":"quiz","title":"April 1977","text":"Activists across the US demand that the 504 rules be signed. In San Francisco about 150 people occupy a federal building, with no ramps, beds or medicine ready for them.","move":1,"q":{"q":"How long did the San Francisco sit-in last?","a":["26 days","3 days","About 6 months"],"x":"The sit-in lasted 26 days, the longest occupation of a US federal building at the time. Food came from groups such as the Black Panthers, and a delegation went to Washington."}},{"k":"boost","title":"Section 504 is signed","text":"The 504 regulations are signed. They become the first national civil rights protection for disabled people in the US and the base for the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990.","move":1},{"k":"boost","title":"Inside government","text":"In 1993 Judy becomes Assistant Secretary in the US Department of Education, responsible for special education and rehabilitation. Later she advises the World Bank and the State Department on disability rights worldwide.","move":1},{"k":"barrier","title":"Still not accessible","text":"Even as a senior official Judy meets inaccessible planes, buildings and meeting rooms. She keeps saying that barriers come from choices society makes, not from a person's body.","move":-2},{"k":"info","title":"Telling the story","text":"Her memoir Being Heumann and the film Crip Camp, both from 2020, bring the movement to a wide public. She is still travelling and speaking when she is in her seventies."},{"k":"info","title":"2023: nothing about us without us","text":"Judy Heumann dies on 4 March 2023, aged 75. Her principle lives on: disabled people must lead decisions that affect their lives. Many rules that make schools and transport more open began with her movement."}],"sources":["js/characters/group2.js (heumann)","Judith Heumann with Kristen Joiner, Being Heumann (2020)","Crip Camp (2020)","docs/characters.md"],"flags":["Crip Camp is about Camp Jened; Judy appears in it. Exact count of 'about 150' occupants and the first wheelchair-using NYC teacher claim are from the single-player data and widely reported; a human may want to confirm."],"evidence":[{"id":"e1","y":"about 1952","ev":"Her Brooklyn school principal refused to let her in because a wheelchair was 'a fire hazard'; she had home lessons for years.","src":1},{"id":"e2","y":"1950s to 1970s","ev":"Steps, buses without lifts and inaccessible buildings and planes limited where she could go, and strangers often had to carry her.","src":1},{"id":"e3","y":"1970","ev":"New York's Board of Education refused her a teaching licence; she sued and became the first wheelchair user to teach there.","src":1},{"id":"e4","y":"1960s","ev":"At Camp Jened in the Catskills she met other disabled teenagers and found a community.","src":2},{"id":"e5","y":"1977","ev":"She led the 25-day sit-in in San Francisco that forced the government to sign the Section 504 rules; Black Panthers brought food.","src":1}],"balance":{"comp":39},"look":{"skin":"#f0cfa8","hair":"#4a3322","hairStyle":"long","top":"#3f8f5a","bottom":"#33384a","outfit":"wheelchair","prop":"glasses"}},"keller":{"id":"keller","intro":"You live through touch: letters spelled into your palm, raised dots under your fingertips, a teacher's hand in yours. The world is slow to learn your language, but you read, write and argue more than almost anyone around you.","zones":{"tunnel":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e1","why":"Silence and darkness: before words she could not share what she knew."},"market":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e4","why":"Public voice: editors said her opinions came from her disability."},"bridge":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e2","why":"A teacher: Anne Sullivan opened language and the world for her."},"ladder":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e3","why":"College: with Anne at her side she graduated from Radcliffe with honours."}},"advantage":{"type":"skip","uses":3,"title":"Fingers on the page","text":"Helen read braille, studied several languages and read widely all her life. On a question you know, count it as answered right.","ev":"e3"},"life":[{"k":"info","title":"Tuscumbia, Alabama, 1880","text":"Helen Keller is born on 27 June 1880. At 19 months an illness leaves her deaf and blind. Her family has no way to share language with a toddler who senses everything through touch and smell."},{"k":"barrier","title":"A home without a shared language","text":"Helen makes up around sixty home signs, but nobody can teach her more. Frustration grows, and some relatives suggest an institution. In the 1880s the South has almost no education for deaf-blind children.","move":-1},{"k":"boost","title":"The water pump, 1887","text":"Anne Sullivan, a teacher from the Perkins School who is partly blind herself, arrives in March 1887. On 5 April, with water running over one hand and a word spelled into the other, Helen grasps that things have names.","move":3},{"k":"quiz","title":"Learning words","text":"Helen now learns quickly, from the manual alphabet to braille and later speech.","q":{"q":"How did Anne Sullivan first teach Helen words?","a":["By spelling the words into her palm","By drawing pictures for her","By speaking loudly close to her ear"],"x":"Anne used the manual alphabet, spelling words with her fingers into Helen's hand while Helen touched the objects they named."},"move":1},{"k":"barrier","title":"The Frost King, 1892","text":"At 11, Helen writes a story that closely resembles one by another author. She is questioned by a panel of men at Perkins without Anne present. She had absorbed the tale through hand-spelling and did not remember it.","move":-1},{"k":"boost","title":"Radcliffe College, 1904","text":"Helen graduates cum laude from Radcliffe. Anne spells lectures into her hand, and few textbooks exist in braille, so she often depends on helpers. She is the first deaf-blind person in the US to earn a bachelor's degree.","move":1},{"k":"info","title":"A writer with opinions","text":"She publishes The Story of My Life in 1903 and goes on to write books and essays. She joins the Socialist Party in 1909 and writes about poverty, factory work and why women need the vote."},{"k":"barrier","title":"Praised, but not believed","text":"Newspapers loved her as a symbol, but once she took political positions some editors claimed her ideas came from the people around her. Her reply was that people had praised her until she disagreed with them.","move":-1},{"k":"quiz","title":"Beyond the headlines","text":"Helen wanted fairness for ordinary people, not only fame.","q":{"q":"Which organisation did Helen Keller help found in 1920?","a":["The American Civil Liberties Union","The American Red Cross","The Perkins School for the Blind"],"x":"Helen was a co-founder of the ACLU, which defends people's rights, including freedom of speech. She also supported workers' unions and the NAACP."},"move":1},{"k":"boost","title":"Across the world","text":"With Anne and later Polly Thomson, Helen lectures in more than 30 countries. She raises money for blind and deaf people and visits soldiers who lost sight in war, helping to open schools and services.","move":2},{"k":"barrier","title":"Anne dies, 1936","text":"After almost 50 years together, Anne Sullivan dies. Helen loses her teacher, interpreter and closest friend in a world with very few trained deaf-blind interpreters. Polly Thomson and others carry on the daily work.","move":-1},{"k":"boost","title":"1968 and what lasts","text":"President Johnson gives Helen the Presidential Medal of Freedom in 1964. She dies on 1 June 1968, aged 87. The full record shows a campaigner as well as a famous student, and the organisations she backed still run.","move":2}],"sources":["js/characters/group2.js keller tiles (Tuscumbia, Anne Sullivan, Frost King, Radcliffe, socialism and suffrage, ACLU 1920, travels, Anne's death 1936, Medal of Freedom 1964)","Helen Keller, The Story of My Life (1903)","American Foundation for the Blind Helen Keller Archive"],"flags":["'About sixty home signs' is the commonly cited figure for Helen's pre-Sullivan signs; check against Perkins/AFB.","The Perkins panel at the Frost King inquiry was without Anne Sullivan present (as widely reported); verify before use.","'More than 30 countries' matches the single-player tile; Keller's own count is often given as 35."],"evidence":[{"id":"e1","y":"1882 to 1887","ev":"After an illness at 19 months she was deaf and blind; for years she had no shared language with her family, only home signs.","src":1},{"id":"e2","y":"1887","ev":"Anne Sullivan taught her the word 'water' at the pump and then many more words by finger-spelling.","src":1},{"id":"e3","y":"1904","ev":"She graduated from Radcliffe College with honours, the first deaf-blind person to earn a BA.","src":1},{"id":"e4","y":"1912","ev":"When she wrote about socialism, newspaper editors dismissed her views as products of her disability.","src":2},{"id":"e5","y":"1892","ev":"After her story 'The Frost King' was accused of plagiarism, she faced an inquiry that left her afraid to write.","src":1}],"balance":{"comp":15},"look":{"skin":"#f0d2b0","hair":"#7a5b3a","hairStyle":"updo","top":"#f5f0e6","bottom":"#4a5a7a","outfit":"dress","prop":"book"}},"grandin":{"id":"grandin","intro":"You are Temple, an autistic girl in 1950s America who thinks in pictures and is hurt by sudden noise. Teachers and doctors expect little. Animals, machines and a few people who listen show you where you can do real work.","zones":{"office":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e1","why":"Medical authority: doctors advised an institution, and others decided about her."},"tunnel":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e2","why":"Noise and teasing: sudden sounds hurt, and many classmates stayed away or mocked her."},"market":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e4","why":"Workplace: the cattle industry was a closed men's world for a woman who is also autistic."},"ladder":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e3","why":"A teacher who saw her talent: Mr Carlock channelled her fixations into school projects."},"bridge":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e5","why":"Mother, aunt and mentors gave her chances and patient time."}},"advantage":{"type":"hint","uses":3,"title":"Picture it","text":"Temple pictures a problem in detail, like a film, and spots what does not fit. Remove one wrong answer before you decide.","ev":"e6"},"life":[{"k":"info","title":"Boston, 1947","text":"Temple is born on 29 August 1947. As a toddler she does not speak, is easily overwhelmed by sound and touch, and avoids eye contact. At this time autism is poorly understood."},{"k":"barrier","title":"Institution advised","text":"Doctors tell her parents that she has brain damage and that an institution is the best place for her, as was common then. Her mother Eustacia refuses and looks for other options.","move":-1},{"k":"boost","title":"Speech therapy","text":"With a speech therapist, daily practice and a mother who insists on manners and new experiences, Temple begins to speak at almost four.","move":1},{"k":"barrier","title":"Teased at school","text":"Classmates call her \"weird\" and mock her habit of repeating herself. School is loud, with crowds and unspoken rules nobody explains, and she often has no one to sit with.","move":-1},{"k":"boost","title":"Mr. Carlock","text":"Her science teacher notices her curiosity and gives her projects that fit her visual thinking. For the first time at school, someone builds on what she is good at.","move":2},{"k":"quiz","title":"The squeeze machine","text":"On her aunt's ranch in Arizona, Temple sees cattle stand calmly in a squeeze chute. At 18 she builds a machine to try it on herself.","move":1,"q":{"q":"What does Temple's squeeze machine give the person using it?","a":["Firm, steady pressure that calms the body","Bright light to focus attention","Loud music to cover other noise"],"x":"Deep pressure helps calm her body. Researchers later studied this idea, and many autistic people say pressure or weighted items help them."}},{"k":"info","title":"Degrees in animal science","text":"Temple studies psychology (BA 1970), animal science (MS 1975) and later earns a PhD (1989). Her field is how cattle and other animals perceive and move through spaces."},{"k":"barrier","title":"A woman in the cattle yard","text":"In the 1970s the livestock industry is mostly men who do not welcome a young woman who talks about how cows feel. She is mocked, kept out of sites and has to prove herself again and again.","move":-1},{"k":"quiz","title":"Curved chutes","text":"A rancher shrugs off her ideas. So she draws and builds handling systems and walks through them herself.","move":1,"q":{"q":"What were Temple's curved cattle chutes based on?","a":["How animals see and move, so that they stay calmer","The cheapest way to build fences","Rules from older handling manuals"],"x":"Her designs reduce stress and balking. Facilities in North America have used designs influenced by her work."}},{"k":"boost","title":"Thinking in Pictures","text":"Her books Emergence: Labeled Autistic (1986) and Thinking in Pictures (1995) explain her mind. She says clearly that autistic people think in many different ways, and that her way is not the only one.","move":2},{"k":"info","title":"Seen by millions","text":"The 2010 HBO film Temple Grandin, a dramatised version of her story, and a place on Time's list of influential people bring her message to a wide audience: autistic people need understanding and fitting support."},{"k":"info","title":"Different, not less","text":"Temple teaches at Colorado State University and speaks about neurodiversity and better schooling. Her message: the world needs many kinds of minds, and each autistic person needs different support."}],"sources":["js/characters/group2.js (grandin)","Temple Grandin, Thinking in Pictures (1995), Emergence: Labeled Autistic (1986)","docs/characters.md"],"flags":["Exact age for the squeeze machine (18) and the mid-century 'institution' advice come from the single-player data."],"evidence":[{"id":"e1","y":"1950","ev":"Without speech at three, she was seen by doctors who advised an institution; her mother refused and arranged speech therapy.","src":1},{"id":"e2","y":"childhood and school","ev":"Sudden sounds such as school bells hurt her; she was teased at school and called names for how she spoke.","src":1},{"id":"e3","y":"1960s","ev":"At Hampshire Country School her science teacher Mr Carlock turned her fixations into projects, which led to college.","src":1},{"id":"e4","y":"1970s","ev":"She entered the male-dominated livestock industry and was kept out of some feedlot meetings and harassed by cowboys.","src":1},{"id":"e5","y":"1960s","ev":"Her aunt's ranch in Arizona, her mother and later mentors gave her room to find her path.","src":1},{"id":"e6","y":"1970s","ev":"She designed curved cattle chutes by picturing animals' view; her squeeze machine came from the same insight.","src":1}],"balance":{"comp":16},"look":{"skin":"#f0cfa8","hair":"#8a6b3c","hairStyle":"short","top":"#a63a3a","bottom":"#2b3f66","outfit":"western","prop":"none"}},"kahlo":{"id":"kahlo","intro":"You play a painter whose world is shaped by a body in pain and by hospital rooms. Long stretches of stillness cost you on the board, but a quiet corner and a mirror are where your best work happens. Every step you take is chosen, not rushed.","zones":{"stairs":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e3","why":"Steps: after the 1953 amputation she used crutches, a wheelchair and a prosthetic boot."},"sprint":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e2","why":"Stamina: after the 1925 accident, pain and many operations limited her body."},"quiet":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e4","why":"Work in bed: the easel above her bed and the mirror made her art possible."},"bridge":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e5","why":"Friends and students (Los Fridos) supported her work and her days."}},"advantage":{"type":"shield","uses":3,"title":"Back to the easel","text":"After setbacks Frida kept returning to work, from bed, in a corset, after surgery. A wrong answer does not send you back.","ev":"e4"},"life":[{"k":"info","title":"Coyoacán, 1907","text":"Frida is born on 6 July 1907 in Coyoacán, near Mexico City, to a German-born photographer father and a Mexican mother. Later she says she was born in 1910, the year of the Mexican Revolution, to tie herself to the new Mexico."},{"k":"barrier","title":"Polio at six","text":"Frida gets polio at six. After months in bed her right leg stays thinner. Children call her \"peg-leg Frida\". Her father pushes her to swim and box, which builds the strength others said she would not have.","move":-1},{"k":"boost","title":"A girl among boys at the Prep School","text":"Frida is one of very few girls at the National Preparatory School, where she studies hoping to become a doctor. She joins a clever, rebellious group of friends and learns to argue and laugh loudly.","move":3},{"k":"barrier","title":"17 September 1925","text":"Aged 18, Frida's bus collides with a streetcar. A metal rail injures her badly: spine, pelvis, leg and more. Medicine in 1925 offers long bed rest and corsets. The plan to become a doctor ends. Over her life she has over thirty operations.","move":-1},{"k":"boost","title":"An easel above the bed","text":"Her mother has an easel made that can be used lying down, and a mirror fixed above the bed. Frida paints her first self-portraits there. She says she paints herself because she is the subject she knows best.","move":3},{"k":"quiz","title":"What to paint in bed?","text":"In bed, with a mirror above her, Frida had to choose what to paint. Many of her best-known works come from this time.","q":{"q":"What became the main subject of Frida's paintings?","a":["Herself, in self-portraits","Famous politicians","Landscapes of Europe"],"x":"About a third of her paintings are self-portraits. She used them to show pain, identity and her Mexican heritage."},"move":1},{"k":"info","title":"Diego, Mexico and Tehuana dress","text":"Frida shows her paintings to the muralist Diego Rivera and marries him in 1929. She wears Tehuana dresses and fills her art with Mexican folk art and plants. Her style is her own, not a European copy or what a \"woman painter\" should do."},{"k":"barrier","title":"Henry Ford Hospital, 1932","text":"In Detroit Frida loses a pregnancy in a hospital that offers little comfort. She paints it, honestly and without decoration. Women's bodies and grief were rarely shown in art then, and critics found it shocking.","move":-1},{"k":"quiz","title":"A first for Mexican art","text":"In 1938 Frida has her first solo show in New York. The following year, in Paris, a famous museum buys one of her paintings.","q":{"q":"Which museum bought a Frida Kahlo painting in 1939, the first work by a Mexican artist it acquired?","a":["The Louvre in Paris","The Prado in Madrid","The Tate in London"],"x":"The Louvre bought her self-portrait \"The Frame\" in 1939. It was a milestone for Mexican art."},"move":1},{"k":"boost","title":"Los Fridos","text":"In the 1940s Frida teaches at an art school in Mexico City. When her health makes the trip hard, she teaches in her own garden. Her students, known as \"Los Fridos\", paint with her and share her view of art.","move":3},{"k":"barrier","title":"A leg is amputated, 1953","text":"Circulation problems lead to the amputation of her right leg below the knee. Medicine in 1953 has few options for her pain, and she is mostly confined to bed. She sinks into depression but keeps painting and keeps her friends close.","move":-1},{"k":"info","title":"Casa Azul, 1954","text":"Frida dies on 13 July 1954, aged 47, at the Blue House in Coyoacán. In April 1953 she had attended her first solo show in Mexico in her four-poster bed. Casa Azul opened as a museum in 1958 and her work is known around the world."}],"sources":["js/characters/group2.js tiles for kahlo (polio, accident 1925, Diego Rivera, Henry Ford Hospital 1932, Louvre 1939, amputation 1953, Casa Azul 1954)","Hayden Herrera, Frida: A Biography of Frida Kahlo","Museo Frida Kahlo (Casa Azul), Coyoacán"],"flags":["Louvre purchase: widely reported as the first work by a 20th-century Mexican artist bought by the museum (The Frame, 1939); the wording in the quiz says first by a Mexican artist, as in the single-player data.","Casa Azul museum opening year given as 1958 from memory.","Share of self-portraits (about a third) is approximate."],"evidence":[{"id":"e1","y":"1913","ev":"At six she had polio; her right leg stayed thinner, and children teased her.","src":1},{"id":"e2","y":"1925","ev":"On 17 September 1925 a tram hit her bus; she broke her spine, pelvis and leg, and had many operations over her life.","src":1},{"id":"e3","y":"1953","ev":"Her right leg was amputated below the knee in 1953 after gangrene; afterwards she used crutches, a wheelchair and a prosthetic boot.","src":1},{"id":"e4","y":"1925 to 1926","ev":"During recovery her family built an easel above the bed with a mirror, and she started painting self-portraits.","src":1},{"id":"e5","y":"1930s to 1940s","ev":"Diego Rivera, friends and her students (Los Fridos) supported her; the Louvre bought a painting in 1939.","src":1}],"balance":{"comp":12},"look":{"skin":"#c89a73","hair":"#1a1410","hairStyle":"braids","top":"#c0392b","bottom":"#1f8a5b","outfit":"dress","prop":"flowers"}},"malala":{"id":"malala","intro":"You play a student whose right to go to school is taken by decree, then defended by family, teachers and strangers on the other side of the world. Rules and checkpoints slow you down, and the people who stand with you speed you up.","zones":{"ladder":{"fx":"block","ev":"e1","why":"School: the Taliban closed the way to school for girls in Swat."},"rope":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e3","why":"Safety: she was shot for speaking up about education."},"bridge":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e4","why":"Family and teachers: her father's school and her classmates stood with her."},"market":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e5","why":"Platform: the BBC diary, the UN and the Nobel Prize gave her a worldwide audience."}},"advantage":{"type":"shield","uses":2,"title":"Stay in school","text":"Malala was knocked back, but her family, school and supporters kept her learning. A wrong answer does not send you back.","ev":"e4"},"life":[{"k":"info","title":"Mingora, 1997","text":"Malala is born on 12 July 1997 in Mingora, in the Swat Valley, Pakistan. Her father, Ziauddin, runs a school, and in her family a daughter's education matters as much as a son's. She loves reading and speaking up in class."},{"k":"boost","title":"A family that backs her","text":"In many families in the region girls are expected to leave school early. Malala's parents insist she and her brothers study the same. Debating and public speaking in her father's school give her confidence.","move":1},{"k":"barrier","title":"Girls banned from school","text":"From 2007 the Pakistani Taliban take control in parts of Swat. They ban music and television and destroy schools. In January 2009 they announce that girls may no longer attend school. Malala is eleven.","move":-1},{"k":"info","title":"A diary under another name","text":"Malala writes a diary for BBC Urdu about life under the Taliban. To protect her, it appears under a false name. She describes fear, closed classrooms and how much she wants to learn. Her identity becomes known later."},{"k":"quiz","title":"The pen name","text":"Malala's blog for BBC Urdu was published under a name to keep her safe.","q":{"q":"What pen name did Malala use for her BBC Urdu diary?","a":["Gul Makai","Nur Jahan","Zeba Khan"],"x":"Gul Makai means \"cornflower\" in Pashto. A BBC reporter chose it to protect her identity."},"move":1},{"k":"boost","title":"A national youth prize","text":"After the army pushes the Taliban back, Malala keeps campaigning. In 2011 she receives Pakistan's first National Youth Peace Prize, and she and her father speak up for schools that were destroyed.","move":1},{"k":"barrier","title":"9 October 2012","text":"A gunman boards Malala's school bus and shoots her because she speaks for girls' education. She is 15. She is flown to Birmingham, UK, for surgery and long recovery. Her friends on the bus are also hurt. Content note: violence.","move":-1},{"k":"boost","title":"A voice at the United Nations","text":"Messages arrive from all over the world. Malala recovers, goes back to school in England and, on her 16th birthday in July 2013, speaks at the United Nations. With her father she starts the Malala Fund for girls' schooling.","move":2},{"k":"quiz","title":"Youngest laureate","text":"In 2014 Malala shares the Nobel Peace Prize with Kailash Satyarthi, an Indian child-rights campaigner.","q":{"q":"How old was Malala when she won the Nobel Peace Prize?","a":["17","21","14"],"x":"She was 17 in 2014, the youngest Nobel laureate ever."},"move":1},{"k":"barrier","title":"Lies and threats online","text":"Some online accounts claim without evidence that she is a foreign agent or that the attack was staged. She keeps getting threats. Fame means being judged by people who have never met her.","move":-1},{"k":"boost","title":"Home again, and Oxford","text":"In 2018 Malala visits Pakistan again for the first time since the attack. In 2020 she graduates from Oxford University in philosophy, politics and economics.","move":1},{"k":"info","title":"A pen, a book, a teacher","text":"Malala's fund supports local educators and girls' schooling in countries such as Pakistan, Afghanistan and Nigeria. She says millions of girls are still out of school. Her message: one child, one teacher, one book and one pen can change the world."}],"sources":["js/characters/group2.js tiles for malala (Taliban in Swat, blog, attack 2012, UN 2013, Nobel 2014, Oxford 2020)","Malala Yousafzai with Christina Lamb, I Am Malala (2013)","nobelprize.org: Malala Yousafzai","Malala Fund (malala.org)"],"flags":["Station 6: name of the 2011 award (National Youth Peace Prize, Pakistan) taken from the single-player data; official title may be National Peace Award for Youth.","Malala Fund focus countries (Pakistan, Afghanistan, Nigeria) listed from memory; check current fund pages.","Gul Makai meaning given as cornflower; this is a Pashto folk heroine name, check wording."],"evidence":[{"id":"e1","y":"2009","ev":"In January 2009 the Taliban in Swat ordered that girls must stop going to school.","src":1},{"id":"e2","y":"2009","ev":"She wrote a BBC Urdu diary under the name Gul Makai about life under the ban.","src":1},{"id":"e3","y":"2012","ev":"On 9 October 2012 a gunman shot her on her school bus; she was treated in Birmingham, UK.","src":2},{"id":"e4","y":"1997 onwards","ev":"Her father Ziauddin ran the school and backed her; teachers, classmates and strangers around the world stood with her.","src":1},{"id":"e5","y":"2013 to 2014","ev":"She spoke at the United Nations in July 2013 and won the Nobel Peace Prize in 2014, aged 17.","src":2}],"balance":{"comp":10},"look":{"skin":"#d9a679","hair":"#2a1d16","hairStyle":"headscarf","top":"#e8a6c6","bottom":"#4a6fa5","outfit":"casual","prop":"book"}},"cox":{"id":"cox","intro":"You are Laverne Cox: a dancer first, then an actor, producer and advocate. Playing this life means moving between stages where you are celebrated and rooms where gatekeepers and strangers decide who you are allowed to be.","zones":{"rope":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e1","why":"Safety: harassment and violence against trans women, which she has described."},"market":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e3","why":"Casting: for years there were almost no roles for trans actresses."},"ladder":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e2","why":"Arts school and college: dance training opened a way into New York."},"bridge":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e4","why":"Her mother, twin brother and community held her up after rejection."}},"advantage":{"type":"lift","uses":2,"title":"Chosen family","text":"Laverne has described how her mother, friends and community lifted her after rejection and insults. After rolling, add 2 to your result.","ev":"e4"},"life":[{"k":"info","title":"Mobile, Alabama, 1972","text":"Laverne is born on 29 May 1972 and raised by her mother, Gloria, together with her twin brother. She loves to dance and perform, and knows from early on that she is a girl."},{"k":"barrier","title":"Told to fit a role","move":-3,"text":"Classmates and some adults bully Laverne for not acting the way they expect a boy to act. She learns to hide, and has spoken openly about how hard this was for her mental health."},{"k":"boost","title":"Dance and a home in the arts","move":1,"text":"Her mother backs her. Laverne attends the Alabama School of Fine Arts, where she studies dance and finds a place where creative kids are taken seriously."},{"k":"quiz","title":"Studying in New York","move":2,"text":"Laverne studies at Indiana University and then graduates from Marymount Manhattan College in New York. In her twenties she transitions and builds a community.","q":{"q":"Which art form did Laverne study at school and at college?","a":["Dance","Architecture","Journalism"],"x":"Laverne trained as a dancer at the Alabama School of Fine Arts and graduated in dance from Marymount Manhattan College."}},{"k":"barrier","title":"No roles, many closed doors","move":-3,"text":"In the 1990s and 2000s there are almost no parts for openly trans actors. Casting people reject her, money is tight, and some employers treat her as a risk or a joke."},{"k":"info","title":"Television and teaching","text":"In 2008 she appears on the reality show I Want to Work for Diddy and then produces and hosts TransAmerican Love Story. She starts using television to explain trans lives to people who have never met a trans person."},{"k":"boost","title":"Sophia Burset","move":1,"text":"In 2013 she plays Sophia Burset in Orange Is the New Black, a trans woman played by a trans actress. The Netflix series is a worldwide success and brings many more people to her work."},{"k":"quiz","title":"A first for acting","move":2,"text":"In 2014 Time puts Laverne on its cover for the story \"The Transgender Tipping Point\". The same year her work for Orange Is the New Black earns an Emmy nomination.","q":{"q":"What was historic about Laverne's 2014 Emmy nomination?","a":["First openly trans person nominated for an acting Emmy","First time a Netflix show was nominated","First Emmy won by a dancer"],"x":"She was the first openly transgender person nominated for an acting Emmy. She also became the first openly trans person on the cover of Time."}},{"k":"barrier","title":"Fame is not safety","move":-2,"text":"Even as a star, Laverne is misgendered, threatened online and drawn into public debates about her identity. She points out that many trans women, especially Black trans women, face this with no platform and no protection."},{"k":"boost","title":"Disclosure","move":1,"text":"She uses her platform on purpose. In 2020 she is an executive producer of the documentary Disclosure, in which trans people look at how film and television have portrayed them for a century."},{"k":"info","title":"A lead on network television","text":"In 2017 she stars in the CBS legal drama Doubt, the first openly trans actor in a leading series role on US network television. She keeps working in film, theatre and production."},{"k":"info","title":"Telling your own story","text":"Laverne keeps acting and speaking out. A theme in her work is that trans people should tell their own stories, on screen and behind the camera, instead of having others tell them."}],"sources":["js/characters/group3.js tiles for Laverne Cox (Mobile, Alabama School of Fine Arts, Marymount Manhattan, Orange Is the New Black, Time cover 2014, Emmy nomination, Doubt 2017, Disclosure 2020)","TIME cover story \"The Transgender Tipping Point\" (29 May 2014)","Netflix documentary Disclosure (2020)"],"flags":["Please check: the Doubt (CBS, 2017) claim \"first openly trans actor in a leading series role on US network TV\" as phrased in the single-player data.","Please check: the Indiana University detail and the graduation wording for Marymount Manhattan."],"evidence":[{"id":"e1","y":"childhood and adult life","ev":"Laverne Cox has told TIME and other outlets that she was bullied as a child and harassed on the street as an adult; violence against trans women is a real risk.","src":1},{"id":"e2","y":"1980s to 1990s","ev":"She trained in dance at the Alabama School of Fine Arts, then studied in New York (Marymount Manhattan College).","src":0},{"id":"e3","y":"1990s to 2012","ev":"For many years there were few roles written for trans women; she kept auditioning and working other jobs until Orange Is the New Black (2013).","src":1},{"id":"e4","y":"1970s onwards","ev":"Her mother and her twin brother raised and backed her; friends and the trans community lifted her after insults.","src":1},{"id":"e5","y":"2020","ev":"The documentary Disclosure, which she produced and appears in, collects trans voices about how media shaped their lives.","src":2}],"balance":{"comp":16},"look":{"skin":"#8a5a3c","hair":"#1a1410","hairStyle":"long","top":"#c0392b","bottom":"#c0392b","outfit":"dress","prop":"none"}},"johnson":{"id":"johnson","intro":"You play a street activist in New York in the 1960s to 1990s with little money, little protection and a lot of friends. Offices and officials rarely help you. The night, the streets and the people there do. Content note: discrimination, poverty, violence and an unresolved death.","zones":{"rope":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e2","why":"Safety: street harassment and police violence against trans women."},"office":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e2","why":"Police and courts: repeated arrests for how she dressed and lived."},"market":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e3","why":"Work: job doors closed to trans women, leaving survival work."},"bridge":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e4","why":"Chosen family: STAR gave shelter to homeless trans youth, and Marsha shared what she had."}},"advantage":{"type":"shield","uses":2,"title":"Family you choose","text":"Marsha's friends gave her a place to sleep and she gave the same to others. A wrong answer does not send you back.","ev":"e4"},"life":[{"k":"info","title":"Elizabeth, New Jersey, 1945","text":"Marsha is born on 24 August 1945 in Elizabeth, New Jersey, one of seven children in a churchgoing family. She loves flowers and bright clothes."},{"k":"barrier","title":"Harassed for her clothes","text":"As a child and teenager Marsha is harassed for wearing girls' clothes. After high school in 1963 she moves to New York City with very little money in her pocket, hoping for more room to be herself.","move":-1},{"k":"barrier","title":"No home, no safety net","text":"In New York Marsha often has no place to sleep. She works as a waitress and in sex work to survive. Landlords and police discriminate against queer and trans people, and there is little help for someone with no address.","move":-1},{"k":"boost","title":"A name and a neighbourhood","text":"Marsha adds a \"P.\" to her name. In Greenwich Village she becomes a familiar, kind and funny figure, known for flowers in her hair and for sharing food and money with those who have less.","move":2},{"k":"quiz","title":"What did the P stand for?","text":"Marsha used the P in her name as an answer to nosy questions about her gender.","q":{"q":"What did Marsha say the P in her name stood for?","a":["\"Pay it no mind\"","\"Peace and love\"","\"Pride\""],"x":"She used \"Pay it no mind\" as a reply to questions about her gender and identity."},"move":1},{"k":"info","title":"Stonewall, June 1969","text":"When police raid the Stonewall Inn, a gay bar in the Village, people fight back, and protests continue for days. Marsha was part of the community in those days. Accounts differ on what exactly she did, and the \"first brick\" story is disputed."},{"k":"boost","title":"STAR House, 1970","text":"With her friend Sylvia Rivera, Marsha founds STAR, Street Transvestite Action Revolutionaries. They rent a building as a shelter for homeless trans and queer youth and pay for it with the money they earn themselves.","move":2},{"k":"quiz","title":"Who founded STAR with Marsha?","text":"Marsha did not build STAR House alone. A friend and fellow activist shared the work and the rent.","q":{"q":"Who co-founded STAR and STAR House with Marsha?","a":["Sylvia Rivera","Audre Lorde","Harvey Milk"],"x":"Sylvia Rivera and Marsha founded STAR in 1970. Both were later left out of much of the mainstream gay rights story."},"move":1},{"k":"barrier","title":"Left out by the movement","text":"Many gay rights groups of the time wanted to look \"respectable\" and kept trans people, drag queens and sex workers at the edge. Marsha and Sylvia were pushed away from events they helped to start.","move":-1},{"k":"boost","title":"Stage and portraits","text":"Marsha performs with the drag troupe Hot Peaches. In 1975 the artist Andy Warhol photographs her for his series \"Ladies and Gentlemen\". Art and performance give her income, friends and an audience.","move":1},{"k":"barrier","title":"The AIDS crisis","text":"In the 1980s many friends die of AIDS while officials and media look away. Marsha is diagnosed with HIV in 1990. She takes part in ACT UP, demanding care, research and respect for the people most affected.","move":-1},{"k":"info","title":"July 1992 and what remains","text":"Marsha's body is found in the Hudson River in July 1992. Police first called it suicide; her friends did not accept that. The case was later reopened and her death remains unresolved. New York announced a monument for her and Sylvia Rivera in 2019."}],"sources":["js/characters/group3.js tiles for johnson (Elizabeth NJ, move to NYC 1963, Stonewall, STAR House 1970, Hot Peaches, Warhol 1975, ACT UP, death 1992)","The Death and Life of Marsha P. Johnson (Netflix, 2017)","New York Public Library archives; Marsha P. Johnson Institute (marshap.org)"],"flags":["Reopened case: police reclassified as undetermined/reopened around 2012; kept general (unresolved). Verify before adding detail.","Marsha's pronouns and identity language: she used both she and he and described herself as a transvestite and gay; text uses she as in the single-player data and focuses on gender identity.","Stonewall role and \"first brick\" claim disputed; text says so."],"evidence":[{"id":"e1","y":"1963","ev":"Marsha moved to New York from New Jersey as a teenager with little money and slept in the streets and with friends.","src":2},{"id":"e2","y":"1960s to 1980s","ev":"Police arrested and harassed trans women and drag performers for how they dressed; Marsha was arrested many times.","src":2},{"id":"e3","y":"1960s to 1970s","ev":"Trans women faced almost total job discrimination; Marsha worked as a waitress and in survival sex work.","src":2},{"id":"e4","y":"1970","ev":"In 1970 she and Sylvia Rivera founded STAR; a house for homeless trans youth followed in 1971.","src":2},{"id":"e5","y":"1975","ev":"She performed with the Hot Peaches troupe and was photographed by Andy Warhol.","src":2}],"balance":{"comp":20},"look":{"skin":"#6b4a33","hair":"#1a1410","hairStyle":"flowers","top":"#e0509a","bottom":"#7a4acf","outfit":"dress","prop":"none"}},"singh":{"id":"singh","intro":"You are Fauja, a farmer's son in a Punjab village around 1911, with legs too weak to walk until you are five. Your running starts in your late 80s in East London, far from home, with a club and a coach who speaks your language.","zones":{"stairs":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e1","why":"Legs: weak legs as a child kept him from walking until about five."},"office":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e3","why":"Papers: no birth certificate, so records could not be certified."},"bridge":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e4","why":"Coach and club: the Sikh community and his coach in Ilford took him in."},"sprint":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e5","why":"Running: marathons from about 89 years old."}},"advantage":{"type":"bestof2","uses":2,"title":"Steady training","text":"Years of daily running meant Fauja could choose his pace and push when it counted. Roll two dice and take the higher one, twice in your game.","ev":"e5"},"life":[{"k":"info","title":"Punjab, around 1911","text":"Fauja is born in a Punjab village. He has no birth certificate, so his exact age is never officially confirmed. His legs are so weak that he does not walk until about five."},{"k":"barrier","title":"Little schooling, no sport","text":"Other children tease him about his thin legs and he cannot join their games. He gets little schooling and no sport, and becomes a farmer like his family.","move":-1},{"k":"boost","title":"Family and farm","text":"He marries Gyan Kaur and raises a family while working the land. In the village he is known as modest and kind, with neighbours he can rely on.","move":1},{"k":"barrier","title":"Losses and a new country","text":"His wife dies in 1992 and a son in 1994. After years of grief, Fauja moves to England to live with another son. In East London he is lonely, and the language is new.","move":-2},{"k":"boost","title":"A club in Ilford","text":"In Ilford he starts running to cope with grief. At 89 he joins a running club and meets his coach Harmander Singh, a fellow Sikh, who also translates and becomes a friend.","move":2},{"k":"barrier","title":"\"Too old\"","text":"Doctors, strangers and even some friends are shocked and say a man his age should rest. People decide what age allows before they ask him. He trains daily, also in cold British weather.","move":-1},{"k":"quiz","title":"The first marathon","text":"In April 2000 Fauja runs his first marathon in London. He finishes, and then goes on to run more marathons, including in New York and Toronto.","move":1,"q":{"q":"About how old was Fauja Singh at his first marathon?","a":["89","65","100"],"x":"He ran the London Marathon in 2000 at 89. He turned 100 (about) in 2011 and finished the Toronto Waterfront Marathon."}},{"k":"boost","title":"A record at 92","text":"In 2003, aged 92, he sets a marathon record for his age group in Toronto. His coach, club and a community cheering at the side of the road are with him.","move":2},{"k":"barrier","title":"No paper, no record","text":"In 2011 he finishes the Toronto marathon at about 100, in just over 8 hours. Guinness World Records does not list it because there is no birth certificate. Many people still honour him, including with a letter from the Queen.","move":-1},{"k":"quiz","title":"Torch bearer","text":"In 2012 Fauja carries the Olympic torch through London at about 100. He also takes part in advertising campaigns and supports charities.","move":1,"q":{"q":"What did Fauja Singh carry through London in 2012?","a":["The Olympic torch","The national flag","A marathon trophy"],"x":"He carried the Olympic torch, and he used his fame to support charities."}},{"k":"info","title":"A message for everyone","text":"His motto: walk, eat simply, be positive. He says age is only a number but also stresses running safely. He ends marathons at 101 with a 10 km race in 2013."},{"k":"info","title":"Home in Punjab","text":"Fauja Singh dies on 14 July 2025 after a road accident in his home village, at about 114 years old. He leaves a reminder: do not say \"you are too old\" before you have asked the person."}],"sources":["js/characters/group3.js tiles for singh","docs/characters.md row for Fauja Singh","BBC and Guardian interviews","Guinness World Records statement on age records","Khushwant Singh, The Turbaned Tornado"],"flags":["Age is unverified (no birth certificate), stated in station 1 and 9.","Station 7 explanation: age at Toronto 2011 is 'about' 100; the 2003 Toronto age-group record is from single-player data.","Stairs zone is an inference from childhood weak legs plus age, not a documented fact; please check."],"evidence":[{"id":"e1","y":"around 1911 to 1916","ev":"He could not walk until about five because his legs were weak.","src":4},{"id":"e2","y":"childhood","ev":"He was a farmer's son in a Punjab village with almost no schooling.","src":4},{"id":"e3","y":"2000s","ev":"He had no birth certificate, so Guinness World Records could not certify his age records.","src":3},{"id":"e4","y":"1992 onwards","ev":"After family losses he moved to Ilford in London, where a Sikh running coach and a club took him in.","src":2},{"id":"e5","y":"2000","ev":"He ran the London Marathon for the first time at around 89, and finished marathons until 101.","src":2}],"balance":{"comp":16},"look":{"skin":"#b8845a","hair":"#f0f0f0","hairStyle":"turban","top":"#f2c230","bottom":"#2b3f66","outfit":"runner","prop":"beard"}},"mandela":{"id":"mandela","intro":"You are a lawyer and organiser in a country whose laws were written to keep you out. Your strength is in numbers, discipline and patience, in a movement that outlasts any one arrest.","zones":{"office":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e1","why":"Law: apartheid laws and banning orders decided where he could live, speak and work."},"rope":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e2","why":"Safety: Sharpeville, arrest and a trial that could end in the death sentence."},"tunnel":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e3","why":"Prison: 27 years apart, with very few visits and letters."},"bridge":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e4","why":"Movement: the ANC, Oliver Tambo and many allies."},"ladder":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e5","why":"Study: Fort Hare, then a law degree by correspondence."}},"advantage":{"type":"lift","uses":2,"title":"Standing together","text":"Mandela's movement, lawyers and supporters carried him through the treason trial and the Rivonia verdict. After rolling, add 2 to your result.","ev":"e4"},"life":[{"k":"info","title":"Mvezo, 1918","text":"Rolihlahla Mandela is born on 18 July 1918 in a village in the Eastern Cape, into a Thembu royal household. A teacher later gives him the English name Nelson, as mission schools often did."},{"k":"boost","title":"Mission school and Fort Hare","text":"He is the first in his family to attend school. He goes on to Fort Hare, one of the few universities open to Black South Africans, and meets future leaders such as Oliver Tambo there.","move":1},{"k":"barrier","title":"Expelled, then on the run","text":"In 1940 he is expelled from Fort Hare after joining a student protest. His guardian arranges a marriage for him, and he leaves for Johannesburg, where he works as a mine guard and a law clerk.","move":-3},{"k":"quiz","title":"1948: a new system","text":"The National Party wins power and turns segregation into the law of the land.","q":{"q":"What did apartheid, introduced in 1948, mean in practice?","a":["Laws separated people by race and limited Black people's rights","A system for sharing land equally between all","A rule that everyone had to learn one language"],"x":"Apartheid sorted people by race. It decided where they lived, worked and studied, and Black South Africans could not vote and had to carry passes."},"move":1},{"k":"boost","title":"A law firm of their own, 1952","text":"After articles with a firm, Mandela qualifies as an attorney. With Oliver Tambo he opens a Black-run law practice in Johannesburg, where clients can get advice that the system rarely offered them.","move":1},{"k":"barrier","title":"Banned and put on trial","text":"From 1952 banning orders restrict where he can go and whom he can meet. In 1956 he is one of 156 people charged with treason. The trial runs for years and ends with all being found not guilty in 1961.","move":-2},{"k":"info","title":"Sharpeville and the underground","text":"Content note: violence. In March 1960 police shoot at a pass-law protest in Sharpeville and 69 people die. The ANC is banned. Mandela goes underground in 1961 and helps start the armed wing Umkhonto we Sizwe."},{"k":"barrier","title":"Rivonia Trial, 1964","text":"Arrested in 1962, Mandela is tried for sabotage with other leaders. He tells the court he is prepared to die for a free, democratic society. In June 1964 the judge sentences him to life in prison.","move":-3},{"k":"boost","title":"The university on Robben Island","text":"Over 18 years on Robben Island, then at two more prisons, the prisoners study, teach and debate. Mandela keeps up daily exercise and, in 1985, refuses release on condition that he give up the fight.","move":1},{"k":"quiz","title":"Counting the years","text":"Mandela is released on 11 February 1990 after international pressure and unrest inside the country.","q":{"q":"How long did Nelson Mandela spend in prison?","a":["27 years","10 years","40 years"],"x":"He was imprisoned from 1962 until 1990, 27 years in all. He was 71 when he walked out."},"move":1},{"k":"boost","title":"1994: everyone votes","text":"He negotiates with President F. W. de Klerk, and the two share the 1993 Nobel Peace Prize. On 27 April 1994 all South Africans vote for the first time, and Mandela becomes president.","move":1},{"k":"info","title":"A promise still being kept","text":"Mandela steps down in 1999 and dies on 5 December 2013, aged 95. Poverty and deep inequality remain after apartheid, and he admits his government's failures, including its slow response to AIDS."}],"sources":["js/characters/group3.js mandela tiles (Mvezo, Fort Hare, apartheid 1948, law firm 1952, Sharpeville, Rivonia, Robben Island, release 1990, Nobel 1993, presidency 1994)","Nelson Mandela, Long Walk to Freedom (1994)","Nelson Mandela Foundation (nelsonmandela.org)"],"flags":["Sharpeville death toll is given as 69 by most sources.","Treason Trial: 156 accused, all acquitted by March 1961; he was first banned in 1952.","Boxing is mentioned in the zone text only generally ('boxing in Johannesburg'); documented in his autobiography.","'Mine guard' at Crown Mines is in his autobiography; check if used elsewhere."],"evidence":[{"id":"e1","y":"1948 to 1952","ev":"Apartheid laws (pass laws, group areas, the 1950 Suppression of Communism Act) limited where he lived and spoke; he was banned in 1952.","src":1},{"id":"e2","y":"1960 to 1964","ev":"At Sharpeville in 1960 police killed 69 protesters; in 1964 he faced a possible death sentence at the Rivonia trial.","src":2},{"id":"e3","y":"1962 to 1990","ev":"After his arrest in 1962 he spent 27 years in prison, 18 on Robben Island, with very few visits and letters.","src":1},{"id":"e4","y":"1952","ev":"With Oliver Tambo he opened the first Black-run law practice in Johannesburg, and he built the ANC's network.","src":1},{"id":"e5","y":"1940s to 1989","ev":"He studied at Fort Hare and later for a law degree by correspondence, even in prison.","src":1}],"balance":{"comp":14},"look":{"skin":"#5a3a28","hair":"#cfcfcf","hairStyle":"short","top":"#d9822b","bottom":"#2c3a2c","outfit":"shirt","prop":"none"}},"beethoven":{"id":"beethoven","intro":"Music is your language, and you hear it more and more from the inside. You live in Vienna among patrons, publishers and friends, in a world built for listeners, and you have to rework how you compose, talk and earn a living.","zones":{"tunnel":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e2","why":"Communication: from about 1818 every talk goes through paper and the conversation books."},"market":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e3","why":"Stage and public life: after 1814 deafness ended his career as a performing pianist."},"office":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e4","why":"Courts: the guardianship fight over his nephew took years of hearings."},"bridge":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e5","why":"Patrons: Archduke Rudolph, Lobkowitz and Kinsky guaranteed his income in 1809."},"quiet":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e6","why":"Inner ear: he sketched at home and composed the late works from imagination."}},"advantage":{"type":"skip","uses":3,"title":"The inner ear","text":"Beethoven composed late works from his inner ear and planned whole structures in his head. Up to three times, a question you know well counts as answered.","ev":"e6"},"life":[{"k":"info","title":"Bonn, 1770","text":"Ludwig is baptised in Bonn on 17 December 1770 into a family of court musicians. His father Johann trains him hard on the keyboard, hoping to present him as a child prodigy."},{"k":"barrier","title":"A household to hold together","text":"Johann drinks, and money is short. After Ludwig's mother dies in 1787 he plays and works as an organist, and he soon becomes the main provider for his younger brothers.","move":-2},{"k":"boost","title":"To Vienna, 1792","text":"Ludwig moves to Vienna, studies with Joseph Haydn and is soon admired as a pianist and improviser. Nobles such as Prince Lichnowsky give him rooms, money and a stage.","move":1},{"k":"quiz","title":"The first sign","text":"Success is arriving when something starts to change.","q":{"q":"What did Beethoven first notice around 1798?","a":["A ringing in his ears (tinnitus)","Pain in his hands","Trouble reading music"],"x":"Around 1798, aged 27, he began to hear ringing and buzzing in his ears. His hearing then faded over the following years."},"move":1},{"k":"barrier","title":"Hiding it from patrons","text":"He fears that a musician who cannot hear will lose patrons, pupils and concerts. So he keeps quiet and avoids parties, which makes him look unfriendly. Nobody around him has any tools for deafness.","move":-1},{"k":"info","title":"The Heiligenstadt letter, 1802","text":"Content note: despair. In a letter to his brothers that he never sent, Ludwig writes about shame, isolation and thoughts of dying. He says his art held him back, and he went on to compose for 25 more years."},{"k":"boost","title":"A new, bolder sound","text":"He writes the Third Symphony (the Eroica, 1804), the Fifth with its four-note opening, and the opera Fidelio. Publishers and patrons keep paying for music that sounds like nothing before it.","move":1},{"k":"quiz","title":"New ways to talk","text":"By about 1818 he can hardly hear speech at all.","q":{"q":"How did visitors talk with the almost-deaf Beethoven?","a":["They wrote in his conversation books","They used sign language","They spoke into a speaking tube"],"x":"Friends wrote their half of the conversation in notebooks; he answered aloud. Many of these books survive and show his daily life, and he also tried ear trumpets."},"move":1},{"k":"barrier","title":"Walls, courts and debt","text":"Concert halls and salons were designed only for listening. Beethoven also fights for custody of his nephew Karl through the courts from 1815 to 1820, with strained money and health. Few friends can talk with him easily.","move":-3},{"k":"boost","title":"The Ninth, 1824","text":"The Ninth Symphony, with its choral Ode to Joy, premieres in Vienna on 7 May 1824. At the end a singer turns Beethoven around so he can see the audience cheering and waving, since he cannot hear the applause.","move":1},{"k":"boost","title":"The late quartets","text":"In his last years he writes string quartets that surprise later composers. They come from what he calls his inner hearing, with friends and copyists who put the score into readable form.","move":1},{"k":"info","title":"Vienna, 1827","text":"Beethoven dies on 26 March 1827, aged 56. Many thousands of people line the streets for his funeral. Today he is played everywhere, and the Ode to Joy melody of his Ninth is the anthem of the European Union."}],"sources":["js/characters/group3.js beethoven tiles (Bonn, Vienna, tinnitus around 1798, Heiligenstadt 1802, Eroica, conversation books, Karl custody, Ninth 1824, death 1827)","Beethoven-Haus Bonn (beethoven.de)","Jan Swafford, Beethoven: Anguish and Triumph"],"flags":["Birth date is not documented; only the baptism on 17 December 1770 is, so the text says baptised.","Custody fight dates (1815 to 1820) are widely cited; check precise end date.","Funeral crowd is often given as 10,000 to 20,000; text says 'many thousands'.","The Ninth is the source of the EU anthem melody (arranged by Karajan); text simplifies this.","The Heiligenstadt Testament was addressed to his brothers and never sent, which is the standard account."],"evidence":[{"id":"e1","y":"1802","ev":"In the Heiligenstadt Testament he wrote that his hearing had been failing for years and that it had pushed him to withdraw from people.","src":1},{"id":"e2","y":"from about 1818","ev":"He used conversation books: visitors wrote their side of a talk, he answered aloud.","src":1},{"id":"e3","y":"1814","ev":"His last public appearance as a pianist in a new work was in 1814; after that his hearing no longer let him perform on stage.","src":2},{"id":"e4","y":"1815 to 1820","ev":"After his brother died he fought in the Vienna courts for guardianship of his nephew Karl, a case that cost him years and money.","src":2},{"id":"e5","y":"1809","ev":"Archduke Rudolph and two princes promised him a yearly income so he would stay in Vienna and keep composing.","src":2},{"id":"e6","y":"1824 to 1826","ev":"He kept sketchbooks and heard music in his head; the late quartets and the Ninth were written deaf.","src":1}],"balance":{"comp":18},"look":{"skin":"#e8c7a0","hair":"#7a7a7a","hairStyle":"short","top":"#2f5d46","bottom":"#c9b99a","outfit":"coat","prop":"score"}},"turing":{"id":"turing","intro":"You are Alan Turing, a mathematician with a stubborn habit of asking what can be computed at all. Content note: this life ends with persecution under an unjust law and an early death; the text stays factual and non-graphic.","zones":{"office":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e1","why":"Law: a 1952 conviction for being gay ended his security clearance and his career."},"tunnel":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e3","why":"Secrecy: the Official Secrets Act forced him to stay silent about his work."},"ladder":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e4","why":"School: Sherborne wanted Latin, not mathematics."},"quiet":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e5","why":"Study and thought: Cambridge gave him time to work out what computing means."},"sprint":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e6","why":"Running: he ran a marathon in 2h46 and trained with a club."}},"advantage":{"type":"hint","uses":2,"title":"Logic first","text":"Turing solved problems by cutting a puzzle down until only the right case remained. Twice, one wrong answer is removed before you choose.","ev":"e5"},"life":[{"k":"info","title":"London, 1912","text":"Alan is born on 23 June 1912. His parents work for the British administration in India, so he grows up with foster families in England and then at boarding school."},{"k":"barrier","title":"A school that wanted Latin","move":-1,"text":"At Sherborne School teachers care about Latin, sport and neat handwriting. Alan cares about science and numbers, and is criticised for his untidy work and for not fitting the school's idea of a good pupil."},{"k":"info","title":"Christopher","text":"Alan's close friend Christopher Morcom shares his love of science. Christopher dies of an illness at 18. Alan is devastated and keeps pursuing science, partly in his friend's memory."},{"k":"boost","title":"King's College, Cambridge","move":2,"text":"Alan studies mathematics at Cambridge and is elected a fellow of King's College at 22, an honour for young researchers. There, people value how he thinks."},{"k":"quiz","title":"The paper of 1936","move":2,"text":"In 1936 Alan publishes a paper that describes an imaginary machine able to carry out any calculation, given the right instructions. It lays the theoretical groundwork for every computer today.","q":{"q":"What is the idea from his 1936 paper called?","a":["The Turing machine","The Enigma machine","The Bombe"],"x":"The universal Turing machine is a theoretical model of computation. Enigma was the German cipher machine, and the Bombe was a code-breaking machine built later."}},{"k":"boost","title":"Bletchley Park","move":2,"text":"In 1939 Alan joins the secret codebreakers at Bletchley Park. He helps design the Bombe to attack the German Enigma cipher, and leads the team working on German naval messages, which protect Atlantic convoys."},{"k":"barrier","title":"A hero nobody could name","move":-1,"text":"The Official Secrets Act keeps thousands of codebreakers silent for decades. Alan gets only a modest honour, an OBE in 1946, and the public does not learn what he did until long after."},{"k":"boost","title":"Running to think","move":1,"text":"After the war Alan runs for the Walton Athletic Club and sometimes runs to meetings. His marathon times are close to Olympic level, and running gives him a way to clear his head."},{"k":"quiz","title":"Can machines think?","move":2,"text":"At the University of Manchester he works on some of the first stored-program computers. In 1950 he asks \"Can machines think?\" and proposes a test with a human judge.","q":{"q":"What do we call his 1950 \"imitation game\" today?","a":["The Turing Test","The Bletchley Test","The Morcom Test"],"x":"The Turing Test asks whether a human judge can tell a machine from a person by conversation alone. It is still discussed in AI research."}},{"k":"barrier","title":"Convicted in 1952","move":-1,"text":"Sex between men is a crime in Britain. After a burglary report, police learn of Alan's relationship with a man and he is convicted of \"gross indecency\". Instead of prison he must accept hormone treatment for a year."},{"k":"barrier","title":"Clearance withdrawn","move":-2,"text":"The conviction ends his security clearance, so he can no longer advise the government on codes. Officials treat him as a risk. He keeps working on mathematics and on how living things grow patterns, increasingly isolated."},{"k":"info","title":"What came after","text":"Alan dies on 7 June 1954, aged 41. Decades later Britain apologised in 2009, pardoned him in 2013 and in 2017 pardoned thousands of other men. Since 2021 he is on the Bank of England £50 note."}],"sources":["js/characters/group3.js tiles for Alan Turing","Bletchley Park Trust; Andrew Hodges, Alan Turing: The Enigma","UK Government apology (2009), royal pardon (2013), Policing and Crime Act 2017 (the Turing law)"],"flags":["Please check: Turing's marathon time and the 1948 Olympic trial claim (he ran about 2h46 in 1947 and was considered for the 1948 Olympic team before an injury).","Please check: wording that he ran to meetings (documented by biographers) and the OBE year 1946.","Cause of death is deliberately not described; the 1954 inquest verdict is not mentioned. Review whether this level of detail suits age 12."],"evidence":[{"id":"e1","y":"1952","ev":"He was convicted of 'gross indecency' under a law that made gay sex a crime; he accepted hormone treatment instead of prison.","src":2},{"id":"e2","y":"1952","ev":"His security clearance was withdrawn, so he lost his work for the secret service.","src":1},{"id":"e3","y":"1939 to 1945","ev":"His wartime codebreaking at Bletchley Park was covered by the Official Secrets Act, so he could not tell anyone.","src":1},{"id":"e4","y":"1926","ev":"At Sherborne School the headmaster wanted classics and Latin; he preferred science.","src":1},{"id":"e5","y":"1935 to 1936","ev":"He was elected fellow of King's College, Cambridge, and wrote 'On Computable Numbers' in 1936.","src":1},{"id":"e6","y":"1947 to 1948","ev":"He ran a marathon in 2 hours 46 minutes and trained with Walton Athletic Club.","src":1}],"balance":{"comp":18},"look":{"skin":"#f0d2b0","hair":"#2a2018","hairStyle":"short","top":"#555a66","bottom":"#3a3a44","outfit":"suit","prop":"none"}},"amira":{"id":"amira","intro":"You play a 16-year-old who loves maths and football and has to rebuild her life in a new country. Much of the way is paperwork and waiting that nobody asked her to carry. Her languages, her team spirit and the people who open doors count for a lot.","zones":{"office":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e1","why":"Papers and recognition: foreign diplomas and residence status are decided slowly, whatever your effort."},"tunnel":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e2","why":"Arriving without the language: for a while you understand little, and few people understand you."},"ladder":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e3","why":"School tracks: pupils with a migrant background more often land on lower tracks at equal marks."},"sprint":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e4","why":"Sport club: football needs no shared language, and clubs are a common first home for refugee children."},"bridge":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e5","why":"Teachers, neighbours and volunteers open doors for newly arrived families."}},"advantage":{"type":"lift","uses":2,"title":"A helping hand","text":"Neighbours, a teacher and a language buddy opened doors for Amira in a new country. After rolling, add 2 to your result.","ev":"e5"},"life":[{"k":"info","title":"Home (fictional)","text":"Amira is an invented character, based on what many refugees report. She is 16, loves maths and football. Her father teaches, her mother is a nurse. Then war reaches her city and damages the school and the hospital."},{"k":"barrier","title":"Leaving everything","text":"Her family decides to leave. They can only take what they can carry. Amira says goodbye to friends, her grandmother and her school books.","move":-1},{"k":"barrier","title":"Months on the road","text":"The journey takes months and depends on strangers, closed borders and long waits. Amira keeps her little brother calm by telling him stories.","move":-1},{"k":"boost","title":"Kindness at a border camp","text":"A volunteer doctor treats her brother. A teacher gives Amira a notebook, so she can keep doing maths. Small acts of help carry a family a long way.","move":1},{"k":"info","title":"Arrival in Germany","text":"The family lives in a shared accommodation and waits for months for papers and a school place. Everything is new: the language, the food, the rules, the weather."},{"k":"quiz","title":"Why can't her parents work?","text":"In Germany her father drives a delivery van. Her mother cannot work as a nurse. Why?","move":1,"q":{"q":"Why can Amira's parents not work as a teacher and a nurse?","a":["Their foreign qualifications are not recognised yet","They do not want to work","Refugees are never allowed to work"],"x":"Recognising foreign diplomas is slow and complicated in many countries. The skills are there, the system is the barrier."}},{"k":"barrier","title":"A language she doesn't speak yet","text":"In class Amira understands little. She is quick at home, but here some classmates see only a girl who cannot speak. A few tease her scarf.","move":-1},{"k":"boost","title":"A teacher and Jana","text":"A teacher stays after class to practise German with her. A classmate, Jana, invites her to football. Amira starts to feel she belongs somewhere.","move":3},{"k":"barrier","title":"Waiting for a decision","text":"The family waits years for a decision on their status. Plans for study, work and travel stay on hold, because the rules decide, not effort.","move":-1},{"k":"quiz","title":"Which school track?","text":"A counsellor suggests a shorter, practical path. Amira wants to study medicine. Studies look at this pattern.","move":1,"q":{"q":"What do studies find about pupils from migrant families with the same grades?","a":["They are more often advised to take lower school tracks","They are always advised to take the highest track","Their track advice is identical to everyone else's"],"x":"Research on school recommendations in Germany shows a bias against migrant families. Mentors and teachers can help push back."}},{"k":"boost","title":"Her Abitur","text":"With extra tutoring and a mentor, Amira insists on the Abitur and passes it. Her parents are proud, and she is accepted to study medicine.","move":2},{"k":"boost","title":"Interpreting at the clinic","text":"Amira volunteers as an interpreter for new arrivals. She knows how it feels to have nobody who understands you. She now feels at home in two places.","move":2}],"sources":["Fictional character: js/characters/group4.js (Amira) and docs/characters.md","UNHCR reports on refugee education","OECD: Education in Germany, students with a migrant background","Federal Recognition Act (Anerkennungsgesetz) 2012, anerkennung-in-deutschland.de","DOSB programme 'Integration durch Sport' (dosb.de)"],"flags":["The track-advice quiz states a widely reported finding about Germany; check the exact wording against an OECD/IQB source."],"evidence":[{"id":"e1","y":"typical case","ev":"Refugees in Germany often wait months or years for residence decisions, and foreign diplomas have to pass a recognition procedure before they count.","src":3},{"id":"e2","y":"typical case","ev":"Newly arrived pupils often sit in class before they speak German; this isolates them even when they are strong in maths or sport.","src":2},{"id":"e3","y":"typical case","ev":"At the same performance level, pupils with a migrant background are placed in lower school tracks more often (OECD, German school studies).","src":2},{"id":"e4","y":"typical case","ev":"Sports clubs are one of the most common first places where refugee children find friends; the DOSB has run integration programmes since 1989.","src":4},{"id":"e5","y":"typical case","ev":"Teachers, volunteers and neighbours are one of the most important factors for refugee children arriving in a new country (UNHCR).","src":1}],"balance":{"comp":22},"look":{"skin":"#d9a679","hair":"#3b2a20","hairStyle":"headscarf","top":"#3a8f85","bottom":"#3b4a6b","outfit":"casual","prop":"none"}},"jonas":{"id":"jonas","intro":"You play a 58-year-old logistics manager who is suddenly job-hunting after 28 years. The hard part is not what he can do but what employers assume about his age. His experience and his network are real assets.","zones":{"market":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e1","why":"Work: ageist assumptions decide before your skills are read."},"office":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e2","why":"Law: age discrimination is banned, but very hard to prove."},"tunnel":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e3","why":"Digital hiring: portals and tests are built for younger applicants."},"ladder":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e4","why":"Further training: older workers are less often offered courses."},"bridge":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e5","why":"A network across generations: tips and skill swaps with colleagues."}},"advantage":{"type":"skip","uses":3,"title":"Seen it before","text":"After decades in logistics Jonas has met most problems before. Up to three times, a question counts as answered right because he has simply done it.","ev":"e6"},"life":[{"k":"info","title":"Monday morning (fictional)","text":"Jonas is an invented character. He is 58, a logistics manager with 28 years in one company, well liked and reliable. Then his company is taken over."},{"k":"barrier","title":"Redundancy","text":"Jonas is told his position is being cut as part of a restructuring. Younger, cheaper staff stay. He has done nothing wrong.","move":-2},{"k":"info","title":"The first search","text":"He sends dozens of applications. Many get no reply. At interviews he is asked how he feels about reporting to someone younger."},{"k":"barrier","title":"\"Overqualified\"","text":"Employers assume older workers cost more, adapt less or leave sooner. Jonas has the skills, but the stereotype reaches the desk before he does.","move":-1},{"k":"quiz","title":"A word for it","text":"What Jonas meets has a name, which the WHO uses in its global report.","move":1,"q":{"q":"What is prejudice or discrimination based on age called?","a":["Ageism","Seniority","Agility"],"x":"Ageism means stereotypes and unfair treatment because of age. The WHO's 2021 global report treats it as a widespread problem."}},{"k":"barrier","title":"Online forms and tests","text":"Long web forms and tests are new to Jonas. Without help he feels lost, and there is no box where 28 years of experience can show.","move":-1},{"k":"boost","title":"A tip from Mira","text":"A former colleague, Mira, hears of an opening in a smaller firm and passes it on. Personal networks often count more than the formal process.","move":1},{"k":"barrier","title":"The oldest in class","text":"Jonas retrains for a new field. Some classmates are friendly. Others assume he will not keep up. Having to prove himself again wears him down.","move":-1},{"k":"boost","title":"Swapping skills with Aylin","text":"A young classmate, Aylin, shows him new software. He shows her how to lead a team. Both of them leave the course better off.","move":1},{"k":"quiz","title":"Mixed-age teams","text":"Jonas's next team has people from many age groups.","move":1,"q":{"q":"What does research usually say about teams of mixed ages?","a":["They work well, since each age group brings different strengths","They always work worse","Only the youngest members matter"],"x":"Studies suggest age-diverse teams do well, because experience and fresh ideas complement each other."}},{"k":"boost","title":"A new role","text":"A company hires Jonas as an operations coordinator. His new manager says his experience is exactly what the team has been missing.","move":2},{"k":"info","title":"Not too old","text":"Jonas now trains younger colleagues. He says: \"Nobody warned me that you can be treated as too old long before you feel old.\" He backs hiring that looks at skills, not birth years."}],"sources":["Fictional character: js/characters/group4.js (Jonas) and docs/characters.md","WHO Global report on ageism (2021)","OECD: Working Better with Age","Federal Anti-Discrimination Agency, Germany"],"evidence":[{"id":"e1","y":"2021","ev":"The WHO reports that about one in two people hold ageist attitudes, and that ageism harms people's chances at work.","src":1},{"id":"e2","y":"2006","ev":"Germany's General Equal Treatment Act bans age discrimination in hiring, but applicants rarely can prove it.","src":3},{"id":"e3","y":"2010s","ev":"Online application portals, tests and video interviews are harder for older applicants (OECD, Working Better with Age).","src":2},{"id":"e4","y":"2010s","ev":"Workers over 55 take part in further training less often, and are less often offered it (OECD).","src":2},{"id":"e5","y":"2010s","ev":"Mixed-age teams share knowledge both ways; older workers often hold the networks and know-how.","src":2},{"id":"e6","y":"2010s","ev":"Long experience lets older professionals solve familiar problems faster (OECD).","src":2}],"balance":{"comp":40},"look":{"skin":"#e8c7a0","hair":"#9a9a9a","hairStyle":"short","top":"#3f6fb0","bottom":"#2f3440","outfit":"shirt-tie","prop":"none"}},"lena":{"id":"lena","intro":"You play a young woman who uses a wheelchair and wants to be an architect in a small German town. Steps, cobblestones and forms stand in her way, and her eye for space and design is a real skill. Playing her means seeing how much of the world is built for one kind of body.","zones":{"stairs":{"fx":"block","ev":"e1","why":"Steps: many buildings and stations are still closed to wheelchair users."},"ladder":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e2","why":"School: many disabled pupils are steered to special schools."},"market":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e4","why":"Work: disabled applicants are invited to fewer interviews."},"ramp":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e5","why":"Accessible design: the ramp route is the one built for you, and you know how it works."},"bridge":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e6","why":"Allies: friends, a teacher and an inclusive employer."}},"advantage":{"type":"lift","uses":2,"title":"Plan B ready","text":"Lena's team and her habit of planning an alternative entrance move her forward. After rolling, add 2 to your result.","ev":"e6"},"life":[{"k":"info","title":"A small town (fictional)","text":"Lena is an invented character. She is 12, uses a wheelchair after a spinal injury as a child, and loves drawing houses and bridges. The old market square is cobbled, and many shops have steps."},{"k":"barrier","title":"Which school?","text":"The nearest school has no lift. The special school is an hour away by bus. Her parents fight for months to get a place at the local school with a ramp and support.","move":-3},{"k":"boost","title":"Friends and Ms Berger","text":"At her new school classmates quickly stop noticing the chair. Her teacher, Ms Berger, makes sure class trips go to places Lena can reach.","move":1},{"k":"barrier","title":"Left out of the trip","text":"In year 9 the trip goes to a hostel with no lift. Lena is told she can \"stay home or be carried\". Her friends refuse to go until the plans change.","move":-1},{"k":"quiz","title":"Who else uses a ramp?","text":"Lena notices that step-free access helps more people than just wheelchair users.","move":1,"q":{"q":"Which group also benefits from a step-free entrance?","a":["Parents with prams and people with heavy luggage","Nobody else","Only people who run marathons"],"x":"Step-free design helps parents, older people, cyclists and delivery staff. Many accessible features are useful for everyone."}},{"k":"barrier","title":"Forms for university","text":"Lena applies to study architecture. Some lecture halls and studios are not accessible. Support and technical aids mean many forms and months of waiting.","move":-3},{"k":"boost","title":"A flat of her own","text":"After 40 viewings, many landlords saying \"it isn't suitable\" before she even visits, Lena finds a small accessible flat near the university.","move":1},{"k":"quiz","title":"The professor's question","text":"A professor asks whether she can do site visits. She answers with a plan: site photos, drones, a partner on site.","move":1,"q":{"q":"What is the main problem on an inaccessible building site?","a":["Missing ramps and step-free access","The wheelchair itself","Lena's ability to draw"],"x":"The barrier is the missing access, not the person. Flexible solutions and adapted workplaces help everyone."}},{"k":"boost","title":"Her degree","text":"Lena graduates with a design for an accessible community centre. Her professor calls it the best of the year.","move":1},{"k":"barrier","title":"Interviews","text":"At job interviews Lena is asked about her \"limitations\" and the cost of adapting the office. Several offices have no step-free entrance.","move":-2},{"k":"boost","title":"An inclusive firm","text":"A mid-sized firm hires Lena and adapts her workspace within two weeks. Wider doors and lower lift buttons help the whole team.","move":1},{"k":"info","title":"Designing for everyone","text":"Lena helps plan a market square with level paths, then opens her own studio for accessible design. She says: \"I'm not stuck in a wheelchair. I'm stuck with stairs.\""}],"sources":["Fictional character: js/characters/group4.js (Lena) and docs/characters.md","UN Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities","Aktion Mensch: inclusion studies","DIN 18040: German standard for accessible construction"],"evidence":[{"id":"e1","y":"2009 and today","ev":"Germany ratified the UN disability rights convention in 2009, yet many buildings and stations are still not step-free.","src":1},{"id":"e2","y":"today","ev":"Many pupils with disabilities in Germany still attend special schools instead of mainstream classes (inclusion studies).","src":2},{"id":"e3","y":"today","ev":"Applications for support and for university exam adjustments take many forms and long waits.","src":2},{"id":"e4","y":"today","ev":"Disabled applicants are invited to fewer interviews than others with the same skills (German inclusion studies).","src":2},{"id":"e5","y":"today","ev":"Standard DIN 18040 sets rules for barrier-free building; architects who know it design accessible places.","src":3},{"id":"e6","y":"typical case","ev":"Friends, one supportive teacher and an inclusive employer change what disabled people can do.","src":2}],"balance":{"comp":15},"look":{"skin":"#f0d2b0","hair":"#8a5a2a","hairStyle":"ponytail","top":"#e07a3a","bottom":"#33384a","outfit":"wheelchair","prop":"none"}},"sam":{"id":"sam","intro":"You are Sam, a fictional 15-year-old in Germany who goes by they/them. Playing this life means noticing how many forms, rooms and routines assume only two choices, and how much a single person who gets it right can change.","zones":{"office":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e1","why":"Forms and law: for years the paperwork had only two boxes."},"tunnel":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e3","why":"School climate: jokes and exclusion at school isolate."},"ladder":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e4","why":"School routines: sports, toilets and trips are split in two groups."},"bridge":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e2","why":"One adult who gets it: acceptance changes a young person's chances a lot."}},"advantage":{"type":"lift","uses":2,"title":"Allies in the room","text":"Sam's friends and a supportive team make the difference when a form or a classroom only offers two boxes. After rolling, add 2 to your result.","ev":"e2"},"life":[{"k":"info","title":"Sam (fictional character)","text":"Sam is a fictional character. Sam was named Samuel at birth, and \"boy\" never quite fitted, nor did \"girl\". At 15 Sam has chosen the name Sam and the pronouns they/them, and has told a few close friends."},{"k":"barrier","title":"Two boxes only","move":-1,"text":"School forms say \"male\" or \"female\". Teams and changing rooms split in two. The class register still lists the old name, and substitute teachers read it out loud."},{"k":"boost","title":"Mr Okafor and Noor","move":1,"text":"A teacher, Mr Okafor, asks Sam privately which name to use and then simply uses it. Sam's friend Noor says, \"I've got you\", and sits next to Sam at lunch."},{"k":"barrier","title":"Jokes in the hallway","move":-1,"text":"A few classmates whisper and make jokes. Sam skips PE and eats lunch in the library. It is not the identity that is hard, but the reactions of other people."},{"k":"info","title":"A letter to the family","text":"Telling the family is Sam's own decision, and nobody should be forced to do it. Sam writes a letter for their parents. They are shocked at first, then start to ask questions and read."},{"k":"quiz","title":"What helps young people","move":2,"text":"Sam looks up what researchers have found about LGBTQ+ young people and wellbeing, and finds that what family and friends do makes a big difference.","q":{"q":"Which factor is among the strongest for the wellbeing of LGBTQ+ young people?","a":["Being accepted by family and friends","Keeping the identity secret","Avoiding school events"],"x":"Studies such as those of the Trevor Project find that acceptance and support, using the right name and pronouns, are linked with better mental health."}},{"k":"barrier","title":"\"It's just a phase\"","move":-1,"text":"Sam's grandfather says it is only a phase. That stings. Sam's mother calmly keeps using Sam's name in front of him, and does not let the subject drop."},{"k":"boost","title":"A youth group","move":1,"text":"Sam finds a local youth group for queer young people. Nobody has to explain themselves there, and Sam laughs more than in months. Some members become close friends."},{"k":"quiz","title":"A third entry in the law","move":2,"text":"Sam learns that Germany's Federal Constitutional Court ruled in 2017 that the civil register must allow more than \"male\" and \"female\". The law changed in 2018.","q":{"q":"Which option did Germany add to civil records in 2018?","a":["A third entry, \"divers\"","A ban on gender entries","An entry for under-18s only"],"x":"Since 2018 the German civil register allows \"divers\" as a third entry, after a ruling in 2017. Many forms and systems are still not updated."}},{"k":"barrier","title":"Forms and offices","move":-1,"text":"At 18 Sam wants documents that match who they are. Many forms still have two options, and every office needs it explained again. Sam learns which rules apply and brings a friend to appointments."},{"k":"boost","title":"An apprenticeship that listens","move":2,"text":"In a bike repair apprenticeship, the boss asks about pronouns, puts the right name on the staff list and trains the team. Sam says that it sounds small, but changed how every day feels."},{"k":"info","title":"Just Sam","text":"Sam fixes bikes, bakes and plays music. Sam would like to be seen as a person with a name, not as a box. Sometimes Sam is asked to speak at schools, and most questions are \"How can I support my friend?\""}],"sources":["js/characters/group4.js tiles for Sam (fictional character, invented scenes)","Federal Constitutional Court of Germany, 2017 ruling on a third gender entry; 2018 introduction of \"divers\"","Trevor Project research on acceptance and wellbeing"],"flags":["Fictional: station 1 says so. Please check whether the 2024 German Self-Determination Act (name and gender-entry changes by declaration) should be mentioned in station 10; it was left out to stay close to the single-player data."],"evidence":[{"id":"e1","y":"2017 to 2018","ev":"Germany's Federal Constitutional Court ruled in 2017 that civil status must allow a third entry; 'divers' was introduced in 2018. Before that, forms offered two boxes.","src":1},{"id":"e2","y":"research","ev":"Young LGBTQ people with at least one accepting adult report better mental health (Trevor Project, a US study).","src":2},{"id":"e3","y":"research","ev":"Trans and non-binary young people report more bullying and exclusion at school (Trevor Project).","src":2},{"id":"e4","y":"typical case","ev":"School routines such as sports teams, toilets and trips are often split into two groups.","src":2}],"balance":{"comp":22},"look":{"skin":"#e8c7a0","hair":"#6b4acf","hairStyle":"short","top":"#3aa39a","bottom":"#33384a","outfit":"hoodie","prop":"none"}},"hawking":{"id":"hawking","intro":"You are Stephen, a physicist in Cambridge whose work takes place in your head and on a blackboard, and whose way to the lecture hall is full of stairs. People around you make a lot of your work possible, and some do not wait to hear you finish a sentence.","zones":{"stairs":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e2","why":"Steps and old buildings: in a wheelchair he often depended on help to get into buildings."},"tunnel":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e3","why":"Voice: after 1985 every sentence had to be spelled out, so every talk takes much longer."},"bridge":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e4","why":"Carers, students and colleagues made his daily life and research possible."},"quiet":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e5","why":"Thinking in his head: he trained himself to see equations without paper."}},"advantage":{"type":"hint","uses":2,"title":"Right question","text":"Stephen's strength was asking the right question and narrowing a huge problem down. Remove one wrong answer before you choose.","ev":"e5"},"life":[{"k":"info","title":"Oxford, 1942","text":"Stephen is born on 8 January 1942 in Oxford to two scientists. At school in St Albans he loves board games and taking things apart, and classmates jokingly call him \"Einstein\"."},{"k":"info","title":"Cambridge, 1962","text":"Stephen starts a PhD in cosmology. In 1963, aged 21, he is diagnosed with motor neurone disease, and doctors expect him to live about two more years. He keeps studying and working."},{"k":"barrier","title":"Old buildings, no ramps","text":"As he starts using sticks and then a wheelchair, Cambridge's old buildings turn into obstacles: steps, narrow doors, no lifts. Everyday tasks need planning and help from others.","move":-1},{"k":"boost","title":"People around him","text":"He gets engaged to Jane Wilde and they marry in 1965. Friends, family and fellow students help with daily life, which leaves more of his energy for physics.","move":1},{"k":"quiz","title":"Black holes leak","text":"In 1974 Stephen shows that black holes are not completely black. At 32 he becomes one of the youngest Fellows of the Royal Society.","move":1,"q":{"q":"What did Stephen show about black holes in 1974?","a":["They slowly give off energy, now called Hawking radiation","They can never form","They are the same as white dwarf stars"],"x":"Hawking radiation means black holes slowly lose energy and can eventually evaporate. It was a surprise to many physicists."}},{"k":"quiz","title":"Newton's old chair","text":"In 1979 Stephen takes up a famous professorship at Cambridge and keeps it for 30 years.","move":1,"q":{"q":"Which professorship, once held by Isaac Newton, did Stephen hold?","a":["Lucasian Professor of Mathematics","Astronomer Royal","Master of Trinity College"],"x":"The Lucasian chair of mathematics was created in 1663. Newton held it before Stephen did."}},{"k":"barrier","title":"Geneva, 1985","text":"Severe pneumonia during a trip to Switzerland leads to a tracheotomy, and Stephen can no longer speak. He needs round-the-clock nursing and a new way to communicate, and nobody has built one for him yet.","move":-3},{"k":"boost","title":"A voice from a computer","text":"A program called Equalizer lets him choose words with a hand clicker, later with a sensor on his cheek. A speech synthesiser speaks them. He keeps its American-accented voice because it feels like his own.","move":1},{"k":"barrier","title":"Spoken over","text":"Many people who use speech devices report that strangers talk to their carer instead of to them, or assume they understand less. Patience would cost nothing. (A common experience, not a documented event.)","move":-1},{"k":"boost","title":"A Brief History of Time","text":"His 1988 book explains the universe to everyone and sells over 25 million copies. A scientist who cannot speak aloud becomes one of the best-known voices on Earth.","move":1},{"k":"info","title":"Paralympics, 2012","text":"Stephen speaks at the opening of the London Paralympics. He urges people to look up at the stars and not down at their feet, and to be curious. He also speaks for access to healthcare and technology for disabled people."},{"k":"info","title":"2018: Westminster Abbey","text":"Stephen Hawking dies on 14 March 2018, aged 76, 55 years after the doctors' first forecast. His ashes are placed in Westminster Abbey near Newton and Darwin. His work belongs to a whole team of people."}],"sources":["js/characters/hawking.js","docs/characters.md","Stephen Hawking, A Brief History of Time (1988) and My Brief History (2013)"],"flags":["Paralympics 2012 speech wording is paraphrased; docs/characters.md says to verify the exact quote.","Station 'Spoken over' is a general experience, not a dated event; marked as such in the text."],"evidence":[{"id":"e1","y":"1963 to 1964","ev":"At 21 he was diagnosed with motor neurone disease (ALS) and told he might have about two years.","src":2},{"id":"e2","y":"late 1960s to 1970s","ev":"He moved from sticks to a wheelchair; many Cambridge buildings of the time were not step-free, so he often depended on help to get in.","src":2},{"id":"e3","y":"1985","ev":"After pneumonia and a tracheotomy he lost his voice; he first used a spelling card, later a computer with a synthesiser.","src":2},{"id":"e4","y":"1960s to 2018","ev":"His wife, nurses, students and colleagues made his daily life and work possible.","src":2},{"id":"e5","y":"1970s","ev":"As writing equations became impossible, he trained himself to hold geometry and equations in his head.","src":2}],"balance":{"comp":20},"look":{"skin":"#f0cfa8","hair":"#5a4630","hairStyle":"short","top":"#6f7b88","bottom":"#2f3440","outfit":"wheelchair","prop":"glasses"}},"parks":{"id":"parks","intro":"You are an organiser long before the famous bus. You type the minutes, investigate cases, pay the poll tax and walk when needed. Most of your barriers are rules written to keep you out, and most of your wins are carried by other people.","zones":{"ladder":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e1","why":"School: no bus for Black children, and she left school to care for her family."},"office":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e2","why":"Voting: she was refused registration twice because of a test and a tax."},"rope":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e3","why":"Safety: threatening calls after the bus arrest."},"market":{"fx":"slow","ev":"e3","why":"Work: she lost her job and could not find work in Montgomery."},"bridge":{"fx":"fast","ev":"e4","why":"Community: NAACP friends and carpools carried the boycott."}},"advantage":{"type":"shield","uses":3,"title":"Friends who stood by","text":"After the family lost their jobs, friends in the NAACP and the church stood by the Parks family. A wrong answer does not send you back; others hold the line with you.","ev":"e4"},"life":[{"k":"info","title":"Tuskegee, 1913","text":"Rosa is born on 4 February 1913 in Tuskegee, Alabama, and grows up in Pine Level with her grandparents, her mother and her little brother. Her grandfather keeps watch at night during Ku Klux Klan threats."},{"k":"barrier","title":"No bus for Black children","text":"In rural Alabama white children ride buses to school while Black children walk. The Black school has a shorter year and fewer resources. Rosa later recalls deciding that this did not have to be the way it was.","move":-1},{"k":"boost","title":"Miss White’s school","text":"At 11 Rosa attends the Montgomery Industrial School for Girls, run by white northern women who teach Black girls that they deserve respect. She takes the lesson to heart.","move":3},{"k":"barrier","title":"Leaving school to care","text":"At 16 Rosa leaves school to care for her ill grandmother, and later her mother. She works as a domestic worker and seamstress, as women of her time were expected to put family first.","move":-1},{"k":"quiz","title":"Raymond Parks","text":"In 1932 Rosa marries Raymond Parks, a barber who secretly raises money for the defence of young Black men falsely accused in a famous Alabama case. He encourages her to finish her diploma, and she graduates in 1933.","q":{"q":"Whose legal defence did Raymond Parks help to fund?","a":["The Scottsboro Boys","The Wright brothers","The Little Rock Nine"],"x":"The Scottsboro Boys were nine Black teenagers falsely accused in Alabama in 1931. Raymond Parks worked in secret to raise money for their defence."},"move":1},{"k":"barrier","title":"The driver who threw her off","text":"In 1943 bus driver James F. Blake orders Rosa to board at the back after paying at the front, and drives away as she steps off. She avoids his bus for years, and she will meet him again.","move":-1},{"k":"boost","title":"NAACP secretary","text":"In 1943 Rosa joins the Montgomery NAACP and becomes its secretary, the only woman in the room. She types, files and records injustices, and in 1944 investigates the Recy Taylor case with the NAACP.","move":2},{"k":"barrier","title":"Trying to register to vote","text":"Literacy tests, a poll tax and intimidation keep Black citizens from voting. Rosa is rejected on her first two attempts. On her third, in 1945, she is registered, after paying years of back taxes.","move":-1},{"k":"quiz","title":"Not the first to refuse","text":"Months before December 1955, a teenager in Montgomery had refused to give up her seat. Activists prepared the ground, and Rosa knew them.","q":{"q":"Which 15-year-old refused to give up her bus seat in Montgomery before Rosa?","a":["Claudette Colvin","Recy Taylor","Coretta Scott"],"x":"Claudette Colvin was arrested in March 1955 and later became one of the plaintiffs in Browder v. Gayle."},"move":1},{"k":"barrier","title":"Arrest and fallout","text":"On 1 December 1955 Rosa stays seated and is arrested and fined $14. She loses her department-store job and Raymond is forced out of his. Threats follow, and the community helps with the bills.","move":-1},{"k":"boost","title":"The Montgomery Bus Boycott","text":"Led by the Women’s Political Council and the Montgomery Improvement Association, about 40,000 Black riders walk, carpool or take taxis for 381 days until the buses are desegregated. The Supreme Court upholds that ruling in Browder v. Gayle.","move":3},{"k":"info","title":"Detroit and the long work","text":"After 1957 the family moves to Detroit, where racism and unemployment follow. From 1965 Rosa works 23 years for Congressman John Conyers and later founds a youth institute with Elaine Eason Steele. She dies on 24 October 2005, aged 92."}],"sources":["js/characters/parks.js (all tiles)","Rosa Parks with Jim Haskins, Rosa Parks: My Story (1992)","Library of Congress: Rosa Parks Papers","Danielle McGuire, At the Dark End of the Street"],"flags":["Raymond Parks and Scottsboro: widely documented, but 1931 date and 'nine teenagers' should be re-checked.","Claudette Colvin arrest in March 1955 and age 15 should be re-checked against a primary source."],"evidence":[{"id":"e1","y":"1920s to 1933","ev":"Black children had no school bus and walked to school; Rosa left school to care for her grandmother and mother and finished later.","src":1},{"id":"e2","y":"1943 to 1945","ev":"She was refused when she tried to register to vote twice; a poll tax and a literacy test were used to keep Black people off the rolls.","src":1},{"id":"e3","y":"1955 to 1957","ev":"After the boycott she lost her job, got threatening calls and moved to Detroit in 1957.","src":1},{"id":"e4","y":"1943 to 1956","ev":"She was secretary of the Montgomery NAACP; community carpools kept the 381-day boycott going.","src":2}],"balance":{"comp":39},"look":{"skin":"#7a5238","hair":"#555555","hairStyle":"bun","top":"#b89a6a","bottom":"#3a3a44","outfit":"coat","prop":"bag"}}};
if (typeof module !== "undefined") module.exports = window.MP.Profiles;
